k publishes on this beat — profiles, analysis and interviews with the people closest to the decisions. New stories are added every week, and the archive below runs to the oldest piece we have filed here.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Have a story for this section? Our editors read every pitch. <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/about/contact/\">Send it to the newsroom</a> — nominations for founders and chief executives are welcome, and so are corrections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Media Coverage','Who is being written about, where, and why it matters.','trash','closed','closed','','media-coverage__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:21:14','2026-10-12 10:51:14','',37,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=41',40,'page','',0),(42,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:03','2026-09-07 06:29:03','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Business Publicity collects the reporting our pr &#038; media desk publishes on this beat — profiles, analysis and interviews with the people closest to the decisions. New stories are added every week, and the archive below runs to the oldest piece we have filed here.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Have a story for this section? Our editors read every pitch. <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/about/contact/\">Send it to the newsroom</a> — nominations for founders and chief executives are welcome, and so are corrections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Business Publicity','Awards, speaking, events and the mechanics of visibility.','trash','closed','closed','','business-publicity__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:21:13','2026-10-12 10:51:13','',37,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=42',50,'page','',0),(43,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:03','2026-09-07 06:29:03','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Global Media Star publishes eight print and digital editions a year. Each is built around a theme rather than a news cycle: a cover interview running to eight pages, four reported company profiles, the quarterly data briefing, and the editor’s note.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Print subscribers receive each edition by post. Digital subscribers can read every issue in full online, including the complete archive below.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','Magazine','Global Media Star in print and digital — every cover, every issue.','publish','closed','closed','','magazine','','','2026-10-12 16:21:15','2026-10-12 10:51:15','',0,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=43',5,'page','',0),(44,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:03','2026-09-07 06:29:03','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The current edition of Global Media Star, available in print and in full to digital subscribers. Print copies ship within three business days of publication.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','Latest Issue','The current edition of Global Media Star, cover to cover.','publish','closed','closed','','latest-issue','','','2026-09-07 12:17:32','2026-09-07 06:47:32','',43,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=44',10,'page','',0),(45,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:03','2026-09-07 06:29:03','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every edition we have published, newest first. Digital subscribers can read any issue in full; single print back-issues are available while stocks last.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','Magazine Archive','Every issue we have published, newest first.','publish','closed','closed','','magazine-archive','','','2026-09-07 12:17:33','2026-09-07 06:47:33','',43,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=45',20,'page','',0),(46,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:03','2026-09-07 06:29:03','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Featured Entrepreneurs collects the reporting our magazine desk publishes on this beat — profiles, analysis and interviews with the people closest to the decisions. New stories are added every week, and the archive below runs to the oldest piece we have filed here.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Have a story for this section? Our editors read every pitch. <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/about/contact/\">Send it to the newsroom</a> — nominations for founders and chief executives are welcome, and so are corrections.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Featured Entrepreneurs','The entrepreneurs who have appeared on our covers and in our features.','publish','closed','closed','','featured-entrepreneurs','','','2026-09-07 12:16:24','2026-09-07 06:46:24','',43,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=46',30,'page','',0),(47,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:03','2026-09-07 06:29:03','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Global Media Star is an independent business magazine covering founders, chief executives and the companies they build. This section explains who we are, who writes for us, how our journalism is funded, and how to reach the newsroom.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','About','Who we are, who writes for us, and how to work with Global Media Star.','publish','closed','closed','','about','','','2026-09-07 12:17:32','2026-09-07 06:47:32','',0,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=47',90,'page','',0),(48,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:03','2026-09-07 06:29:03','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Global Media Star is a business magazine for the people who run companies. We publish reporting on founders, chief executives and the operators one layer below them — the executives who make a strategy actually happen. Our readership is roughly forty thousand across print and digital, concentrated in companies between fifty and five thousand employees.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We are deliberately narrow. We do not cover consumer technology, personal finance or markets commentary. What we do cover, we try to cover better than anyone: how companies are built, what changes when they scale, and the decisions that look obvious in hindsight and were not obvious at the time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What we publish</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reported features.</strong> Long-form profiles of founders, chief executives and companies, built on multiple interviews and, wherever possible, on numbers we have been shown rather than told about.</li>\n<li><strong>Sector analysis.</strong> Weekly reporting from desks covering business, entrepreneurship, startups, marketing and communications.</li>\n<li><strong>The magazine.</strong> Eight print and digital editions a year, each built around a theme rather than a news cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>The GMS Briefing.</strong> A weekly email for subscribers with the stories, data and corrections that matter.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>How we work</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every feature is fact-checked before publication. Subjects see quotes attributed to them, in context, before we run them — they do not see the article, and they do not have approval over it. We do not publish sponsored content inside our editorial sections, and any commercial relationship with a company we cover is disclosed in the piece.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We get things wrong. When we do, we correct them at the top of the article, with the date and a description of what changed. Corrections are not quietly edited in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The test we apply to every story is simple: would somebody running a company change a decision because of this? If not, we probably should not have published it.</p><cite>Eleanor Hartley, Editor-in-Chief</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Independence</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Global Media Star is independently owned. Our revenue comes from subscriptions, print and digital advertising, and a small events business. No advertiser has ever been given sight of an article before publication, and no advertiser has editorial input. Where we accept sponsorship of a section or an event, it is labelled clearly and the sponsor has no say in what appears there.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Get in touch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Story pitches, founder nominations, corrections and complaints all reach a person who reads them. The fastest route is <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/about/contact/\">the contact page</a>. If you would like to work with us commercially, our <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/about/advertise-with-us/\">advertising page</a> has rates and formats.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','About Us','Our mission, our readership and what we publish.','publish','closed','closed','','about-us','','','2026-09-07 12:17:32','2026-09-07 06:47:32','',47,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=48',10,'page','',0),(49,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Global Media Star is produced by a small permanent editorial team and a network of contributing writers. Every name below is reachable — bylines link to an author archive, and pitches sent to a named editor get read by that editor.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The editorial desk</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Eleanor Hartley — Editor-in-Chief</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Eleanor has edited business publications for eighteen years and has run Global Media Star since its relaunch. She commissions the cover features, chairs the weekly conference, and is the final decision on anything contested. She writes rarely and edits constantly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Marcus De Souza — Business Editor</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Marcus runs the business desk: companies, corporate strategy, industry analysis and the reporting that sits underneath the headline numbers. He spent nine years covering mid-market industrials before joining GMS and is the person most likely to ask what a metric actually measures.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Ayesha Iqbal — Startups Correspondent</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ayesha covers the early-stage market — funding, founders, and companies before anyone has heard of them. She maintains the GMS funding tracker and writes the quarterly early-stage review. She is the right person for anything at seed or Series A.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Joseph Linton — Features Writer</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Joseph writes the long-form profiles and cover interviews. His pieces typically run to four or five thousand words and take six to ten weeks. If you are nominating a founder or chief executive for a feature, the nomination reaches him.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\n<h3>Sipho Mbeki — Marketing & Media Editor</h3>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sipho covers brand, demand generation, communications and how companies build reputation. He edits the marketing and PR sections and writes the annual GMS communications benchmark.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Contributors</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We commission freelance writers for roughly a third of what we publish, particularly for reporting outside our core markets. We pay on acceptance rather than publication, at commercial rates, and we buy first serial rights only. If you would like to pitch, read a few recent pieces first and then write to the relevant desk editor with a specific idea rather than a topic.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Corrections and complaints</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Corrections go to the Editor-in-Chief and are handled within two business days. If you believe a piece is materially wrong, say what is wrong and what the correct position is; we will check it and, where we got it wrong, correct it at the top of the article with a dated note.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','Editorial Team','The editors and writers behind the magazine.','publish','closed','closed','','editorial-team','','','2026-09-07 12:17:32','2026-09-07 06:47:32','',47,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=49',20,'page','',0),(50,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Every message sent through this page reaches an editor rather than a queue. We aim to reply to editorial enquiries within two business days, and to commercial enquiries within one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>If you are pitching a story, the most useful thing you can send is a specific idea with a reason it matters now — not a company overview. If you are nominating a founder or chief executive for a feature, tell us what they have built and what would be surprising about the piece.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','Contact','Reach the newsroom, the advertising desk or subscriber support.','publish','closed','closed','','contact','','','2026-09-07 12:17:32','2026-09-07 06:47:32','',47,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=50',30,'page','',0),(51,0,'2026-09-07 06:29:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Global Media Star reaches roughly forty thousand business leaders across print, digital and the weekly briefing. Our readers are decision-makers at companies between fifty and five thousand employees, concentrated in operations, strategy and general management. They are, by some distance, the most useful audience we could have assembled for anyone selling into the mid-market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What we offer</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Print advertising.</strong> Full-page, half-page and inside-cover positions across our eight annual editions. Print carries a long shelf life in this readership; issues are still being read months after publication.</li>\n<li><strong>Digital display.</strong> Section sponsorship and article-level placements, sold by share of voice rather than impression volume.</li>\n<li><strong>The GMS Briefing.</strong> A single sponsored position in the weekly email, with a clear label and a fixed format.</li>\n<li><strong>Branded content.</strong> Written by our commercial team, clearly labelled, and never placed inside editorial sections.</li>\n<li><strong>Events.</strong> Roundtables and dinners, run under the Chatham House Rule, with a guest list built jointly.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What we do not do</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We do not sell editorial coverage, and we do not offer paid features, paid interviews or paid awards. If someone has told you that a placement in our editorial pages can be bought, they were not speaking for us. Advertisers get no sight of articles before publication and no editorial input of any kind.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Audience</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Approximately 40,000 readers across print and digital.</li>\n<li>71% hold director-level roles or above.</li>\n<li>Median company size of 340 employees.</li>\n<li>Readership concentrated across Europe, South Asia, the Gulf and Sub-Saharan Africa.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Full readership data, rate card and production specifications are available on request. Bookings for a given edition close six weeks before publication.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Next steps</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Write to our commercial team through <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/about/contact/\">the contact page</a>, selecting “Advertising”, and tell us what you are trying to reach. We will send the current rate card and, if it is useful, suggest which formats actually fit rather than which are most expensive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n','Advertise With Us','Sponsorship, branded content and print advertising with GMS.','publish','closed','closed','','advertise-with-us','','','2026-09-07 12:17:32','2026-09-07 06:47:32','',47,'http://localhost:8080/?page_id=51',40,'page','',0),(52,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','52','','','2026-09-07 11:59:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04','',0,'http://localhost:8080/52',1,'nav_menu_item','',0),(53,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','53','','','2026-09-07 11:59:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04','',0,'http://localhost:8080/53',6,'nav_menu_item','',0),(54,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','54','','','2026-09-07 11:59:04','2026-09-07 06:29:04','',6,'http://localhost:8080/54',7,'nav_menu_item','',0),(55,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:05','2026-09-07 06:29:05',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','55','','','2026-09-07 11:59:05','2026-09-07 06:29:05','',6,'http://localhost:8080/55',8,'nav_menu_item','',0),(56,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:05','2026-09-07 06:29:05',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','56','','','2026-09-07 11:59:05','2026-09-07 06:29:05','',6,'http://localhost:8080/56',9,'nav_menu_item','',0),(57,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:05','2026-09-07 06:29:05',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','57','','','2026-09-07 11:59:05','2026-09-07 06:29:05','',6,'http://localhost:8080/57',10,'nav_menu_item','',0),(58,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','58','','','2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06','',6,'http://localhost:8080/58',11,'nav_menu_item','',0),(59,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','59','','','2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06','',0,'http://localhost:8080/59',12,'nav_menu_item','',0),(60,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','60','','','2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06','',12,'http://localhost:8080/60',13,'nav_menu_item','',0),(61,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','61','','','2026-09-07 11:59:06','2026-09-07 06:29:06','',12,'http://localhost:8080/61',14,'nav_menu_item','',0),(62,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:07','2026-09-07 06:29:07',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','62','','','2026-09-07 11:59:07','2026-09-07 06:29:07','',12,'http://localhost:8080/62',15,'nav_menu_item','',0),(63,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:07','2026-09-07 06:29:07',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','63','','','2026-09-07 11:59:07','2026-09-07 06:29:07','',12,'http://localhost:8080/63',16,'nav_menu_item','',0),(64,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:07','2026-09-07 06:29:07',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','64','','','2026-09-07 11:59:07','2026-09-07 06:29:07','',12,'http://localhost:8080/64',17,'nav_menu_item','',0),(72,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','72','','','2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10','',0,'http://localhost:8080/72',18,'nav_menu_item','',0),(73,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','73','','','2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10','',25,'http://localhost:8080/73',19,'nav_menu_item','',0),(74,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','74','','','2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10','',25,'http://localhost:8080/74',20,'nav_menu_item','',0),(75,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','75','','','2026-09-07 11:59:10','2026-09-07 06:29:10','',25,'http://localhost:8080/75',21,'nav_menu_item','',0),(76,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:11','2026-09-07 06:29:11',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','76','','','2026-09-07 11:59:11','2026-09-07 06:29:11','',25,'http://localhost:8080/76',22,'nav_menu_item','',0),(77,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:11','2026-09-07 06:29:11',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','77','','','2026-09-07 11:59:11','2026-09-07 06:29:11','',25,'http://localhost:8080/77',23,'nav_menu_item','',0),(90,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:15','2026-09-07 06:29:15',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','90','','','2026-09-07 11:59:15','2026-09-07 06:29:15','',0,'http://localhost:8080/90',2,'nav_menu_item','',0),(91,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:15','2026-09-07 06:29:15',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','91','','','2026-09-07 11:59:15','2026-09-07 06:29:15','',43,'http://localhost:8080/91',3,'nav_menu_item','',0),(92,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','92','','','2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16','',43,'http://localhost:8080/92',4,'nav_menu_item','',0),(93,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','93','','','2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16','',43,'http://localhost:8080/93',5,'nav_menu_item','',0),(94,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','94','','','2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16','',0,'http://localhost:8080/94',24,'nav_menu_item','',0),(95,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','95','','','2026-09-07 11:59:16','2026-09-07 06:29:16','',47,'http://localhost:8080/95',25,'nav_menu_item','',0),(96,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:17','2026-09-07 06:29:17',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','96','','','2026-09-07 11:59:17','2026-09-07 06:29:17','',47,'http://localhost:8080/96',26,'nav_menu_item','',0),(97,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:17','2026-09-07 06:29:17',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','97','','','2026-09-07 11:59:17','2026-09-07 06:29:17','',47,'http://localhost:8080/97',27,'nav_menu_item','',0),(98,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:17','2026-09-07 06:29:17',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','98','','','2026-09-07 11:59:17','2026-09-07 06:29:17','',47,'http://localhost:8080/98',28,'nav_menu_item','',0),(99,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','99','','','2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18','',43,'http://localhost:8080/99',10,'nav_menu_item','',0),(100,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18','','Advertise','','publish','closed','closed','','advertise','','','2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18','',47,'http://localhost:8080/advertise',20,'nav_menu_item','',0),(101,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','101','','','2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18','',47,'http://localhost:8080/101',30,'nav_menu_item','',0),(102,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','102','','','2026-09-07 11:59:18','2026-09-07 06:29:18','',47,'http://localhost:8080/102',10,'nav_menu_item','',0),(103,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:19','2026-09-07 06:29:19',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','103','','','2026-09-07 11:59:19','2026-09-07 06:29:19','',47,'http://localhost:8080/103',20,'nav_menu_item','',0),(104,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:19','2026-09-07 06:29:19',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','104','','','2026-09-07 11:59:19','2026-09-07 06:29:19','',47,'http://localhost:8080/104',30,'nav_menu_item','',0),(105,0,'2026-09-07 11:59:19','2026-09-07 06:29:19',' ','','','publish','closed','closed','','105','','','2026-09-07 11:59:19','2026-09-07 06:29:19','',47,'http://localhost:8080/105',40,'nav_menu_item','',0),(106,2,'2026-08-30 00:14:24','2026-08-29 18:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in logistics software was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop is one of them. Founded in Singapore and run by Priya Raghunathan, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1335,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-bf7da0674c-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1335\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skylines_of_the_Central_Business_District_at_night_in_Singapore.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Priya Raghunathan is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Meridian Loop is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Meridian Loop posts a record quarter as logistics software demand holds','Renewals carried the number, but management flagged a tighter margin path into next year.','publish','open','open','','business-news-9d2c4f94','','','2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-news-9d2c4f94/',0,'post','',0),(108,3,'2026-03-02 20:14:24','2026-03-02 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Halden Grid reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a energy infrastructure market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo, founder at the Lagos-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Halden Grid does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1361,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-f1aa6e1086-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1361\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Water_treatment_plant_in_an_abandoned_steel_factory_in_Oupeye,_Belgium_(DSCF3285).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Trougnouf · CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For energy infrastructure businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Halden Grid’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Daniel Okonkwo is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Halden Grid is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Mid-market consolidation accelerates as Halden Grid closes its third deal','The acquisition is small. The pattern behind it is not.','publish','open','open','','business-news-a7953f4e','','','2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-news-a7953f4e/',0,'post','',0),(110,4,'2026-05-21 02:14:24','2026-05-20 20:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across clinical operations and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Quorum Health, Marta Ferreira moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1362,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-b31965eba4-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1362\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Water_plant_in_an_abandoned_steel_factory_in_Oupeye,_Belgium_(DSCF3282).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Trougnouf · CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in clinical operations are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in clinical operations, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Boards are still budgeting for shocks that have not arrived','Supply chains have steadied. Contingency lines in the budget have not come down.','publish','open','open','','business-news-5c47ec74','','','2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-news-5c47ec74/',0,'post','',0),(112,5,'2026-03-24 00:14:24','2026-03-23 18:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in risk analytics was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics is one of them. Founded in Manchester and run by Ethan Brightwell, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1363,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-ebd4a0a38e-1024x461.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1363\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%BClmen,_Wiesmann_Sports_Cars,_Wiesmann_GT_MF5_--_2018_--_9517.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Ethan Brightwell is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Northgate Analytics is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Northgate Analytics takes its risk analytics platform into three new markets','A deliberate, unglamorous expansion — and a test of whether the model travels.','publish','open','open','','business-news-61abb5a6','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-news-61abb5a6/',0,'post','',0),(114,6,'2026-06-29 20:14:24','2026-06-29 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across consumer manufacturing and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Tallow & Co., Sana Qureshi moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1364,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-795c40de59-1024x389.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1364\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schakelruimte.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ger Hagens · CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in consumer manufacturing are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in consumer manufacturing, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The quiet return of operating discipline','After a decade of growth-first orthodoxy, the companies setting the pace are the ones counting carefully.','publish','open','open','','business-trends-476a2ac3','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-trends-476a2ac3/',0,'post','',0),(116,2,'2026-04-14 20:14:24','2026-04-14 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in freight forwarding was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight is one of them. Founded in Marseille and run by Julien Marchetti, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1365,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-fd8ecda3dc-1024x682.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1365\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Singapore_Cargo-Terminal-01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Julien Marchetti is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Bastion Freight is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Why freight forwarding buyers are consolidating vendors','Procurement is shrinking supplier lists. Challengers are discovering what that costs them.','publish','open','open','','business-trends-a846dd46','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-trends-a846dd46/',0,'post','',0),(118,3,'2026-03-10 04:14:24','2026-03-09 22:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Kindred Payments reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a payments market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng, chief executive at the Accra-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Kindred Payments does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1366,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-fdda5f1f19-1024x682.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1366\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WPAhoi,_Terminal_Burchardkai,_Hamburg_(P1080571).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Matti Blume · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For payments businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Kindred Payments’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Amara Boateng is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Kindred Payments is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Hybrid work settled. Now companies are redesigning around it.','The argument is over. The expensive part — rebuilding how work actually happens — is starting.','publish','open','open','','business-trends-7ea03ef4','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-trends-7ea03ef4/',0,'post','',0),(120,4,'2026-04-26 01:14:24','2026-04-25 19:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across developer tooling and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Sextant Labs, Rohan Vaidya moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1367,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-05ceec19b2-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1367\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portainers,_WPAhoi,_Waltershofer_Hafen,_Hamburg_(P1080572).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Matti Blume · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in developer tooling are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in developer tooling, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Capital is patient again, and it is changing how companies plan','Longer horizons are producing different decisions, and a different kind of company.','publish','open','open','','business-trends-30c26005','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-trends-30c26005/',0,'post','',0),(122,5,'2026-03-02 05:14:24','2026-03-01 23:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Fjordline Materials reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a advanced materials market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim, founder at the Bergen-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Fjordline Materials does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1368,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-1087464baf-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1368\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CMA_CGM_Kerguelen,_Container_Terminal_Burchardkai,_Hamburg_(P1080471).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Matti Blume · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For advanced materials businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Fjordline Materials’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Ingrid Solheim is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Fjordline Materials is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside Fjordline Materials: the operating model behind five years of compounding','No single breakthrough. A hundred decisions that pointed the same direction.','publish','open','open','','companies-072fb3e5','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',0,'http://localhost:8080/companies-072fb3e5/',0,'post','',0),(124,6,'2026-04-16 00:14:24','2026-04-15 18:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across insurance and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Ardent Mutual, Caleb Whitfield moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1369,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-33efe4654e-1024x698.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1369\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Container_Terminal_Tollerort,_Vorhafen,_Hamburg_(P1080405).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Matti Blume · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in insurance are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in insurance, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Ardent Mutual rebuilt its insurance business without losing customers','A replatforming that could have gone badly, executed in public, over three years.','publish','open','open','','companies-4431301e','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',0,'http://localhost:8080/companies-4431301e/',0,'post','',0),(126,2,'2026-03-24 23:14:24','2026-03-24 17:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in industrial robotics was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly is one of them. Founded in Taipei and run by Lin Wei-Chen, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1370,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-81596e3387-1024x577.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1370\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dimitris_Y,_Tankweg,_Hamburg_(P1080472).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Matti Blume · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Lin Wei-Chen is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Orbit Assembly is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Orbit Assembly bets its next decade on services, not licences','The margin profile changes. So does almost everything else about the company.','publish','open','open','','companies-0a427ddc','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',0,'http://localhost:8080/companies-0a427ddc/',0,'post','',0),(128,3,'2026-05-23 23:14:24','2026-05-23 17:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Cellar Six reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a food and beverage market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois, founder at the Montreal-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Cellar Six does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1371,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-43d2a1fa0a-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1371\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WP_Ahoi,_Container_Terminal_Altenwerder,_Hamburg_(P1080529).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Matti Blume · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For food and beverage businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Cellar Six’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Nadia Bourgeois is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Cellar Six is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The org chart that let Cellar Six double without doubling headcount','Structure as strategy: what Nadia Bourgeois changed, and what it cost.','publish','open','open','','companies-bca63205','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',0,'http://localhost:8080/companies-bca63205/',0,'post','',0),(130,4,'2026-02-22 06:14:24','2026-02-22 00:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in building automation was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems is one of them. Founded in Prague and run by Tomas Novak, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1372,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-6441a69292-1024x695.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1372\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Hook_Container_Terminal_New_York_September_2016_002.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">King of Hearts · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Tomas Novak is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Vesper Systems is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Boards are asking harder questions about AI. Vesper Systems has answers.','Governance has caught up with the technology faster than most executives expected.','publish','open','open','','corporate-be83278e','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',0,'http://localhost:8080/corporate-be83278e/',0,'post','',0),(132,5,'2026-06-23 20:14:24','2026-06-23 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Rafiq Capital reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a small-business lending market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive at the Amman-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Rafiq Capital does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive, Rafiq Capital</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1373,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-b83e20cb04-1024x685.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1373\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Hook_Container_Terminal_New_York_September_2016_001.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">King of Hearts · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For small-business lending businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Rafiq Capital’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Aisha Mansour is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Rafiq Capital is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Governance is no longer a compliance line item','The companies treating it as strategy are getting cheaper capital and calmer boards.','publish','open','open','','corporate-24ee5286','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',0,'http://localhost:8080/corporate-24ee5286/',0,'post','',0),(134,6,'2026-04-18 06:14:24','2026-04-18 00:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across agricultural technology and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Baltic Yield, Gregor Lindqvist moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1374,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-5b5f2c9db5-1024x473.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1374\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vancouver_(BC,_Canada),_Vancouver_Centerm_Terminal_--_2022_--_1848.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in agricultural technology are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in agricultural technology, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','What Baltic Yield’s succession plan says about modern leadership','Announced early, rehearsed properly, and unusually specific about what happens next.','publish','open','open','','corporate-50d0ac16','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/corporate-50d0ac16/',0,'post','',0),(136,2,'2026-05-06 21:14:24','2026-05-06 15:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in design manufacturing was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove is one of them. Founded in Milan and run by Chiara Bellandi, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1375,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-e78c424d9e-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1375\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vancouver_(BC,_Canada),_Vancouver_Centerm_Terminal_--_2022_--_2151.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Chiara Bellandi is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Atelier Nove is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside the restructure Chiara Bellandi spent two years arguing for','A reorganisation nobody wanted, that most of the executive team now defends.','publish','open','open','','corporate-b6011065','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/corporate-b6011065/',0,'post','',0),(138,3,'2026-06-10 05:14:24','2026-06-09 23:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across workforce training and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Longbridge Learning, Samuel Adeyemi moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1376,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-f8ebfc8c96-1024x601.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1376\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Container-Terminal_Bremerhaven_01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">H. Zell · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in workforce training are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in workforce training, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Workforce training, mapped: where the margin is moving','Value is shifting down the stack. The companies that noticed first are already repositioning.','publish','open','open','','industry-insights-dcea2c2b','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/industry-insights-dcea2c2b/',0,'post','',0),(140,4,'2026-06-08 06:14:24','2026-06-08 00:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in warehousing was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik is one of them. Founded in Hamburg and run by Hannah Vogel, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1377,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-a63a57e88e-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1377\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20230318_Port_Hamburg_04.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Flocci Nivis · CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Hannah Vogel is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Steinweg Logistik is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Five forces reshaping warehousing — and who is positioned for them','A sector read from the desks that cover it closest.','publish','open','open','','industry-insights-756451b9','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/industry-insights-756451b9/',0,'post','',0),(142,5,'2026-06-19 05:14:24','2026-06-18 23:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Ribera Foods reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a food distribution market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive at the Bogotá-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Ribera Foods does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1378,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-f5202dc109-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1378\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025-08-31_D300-7711_Achim-Lammerts_Mannheim-Rheinhafen.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Achim Lammerts (Syntaxys) · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For food distribution businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Ribera Foods’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Diego Salcedo is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Ribera Foods is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The unglamorous infrastructure quietly deciding food distribution’s winners','Nobody puts it in a pitch deck. It determines who can serve the biggest customers.','publish','open','open','','industry-insights-9829755b','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/industry-insights-9829755b/',0,'post','',0),(144,6,'2026-05-28 02:14:24','2026-05-27 20:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across field service software and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Aster Fieldworks, Meera Krishnan moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1379,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-ec82f9b63c-1024x796.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, logistics and infrastructure\" class=\"wp-image-1379\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IKEA_Anderlecht_self-serve_warehouse_(DSCF3734).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Trougnouf · CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in field service software are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in field service software, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Why field service software incumbents keep losing the middle of the market','Too expensive for the bottom, too rigid for the top. The squeeze is structural.','publish','open','open','','industry-insights-1c44ac7c','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/industry-insights-1c44ac7c/',0,'post','',0),(146,2,'2026-03-07 22:14:24','2026-03-07 16:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is unusually precise about the moment Trellis Property nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Trellis Property employs a substantial team out of Sydney and serves customers across commercial real estate. Oliver Ashworth tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1306,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8336172513-1024x459.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1306\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_the_Central_Business_District_of_Singapore_with_Esplanade_Bridge.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Trellis Property had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Oliver Ashworth would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Trellis Property still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Oliver Ashworth: “We were nine months from closing. Then one customer said yes.”','The story of Trellis Property, told from the point where it very nearly did not happen.','publish','open','open','','entrepreneur-stories-18dad4dc','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/entrepreneur-stories-18dad4dc/',0,'post','',0),(148,3,'2026-08-18 21:14:24','2026-08-18 15:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Fatima Al-Rashid is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Nawa Health was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable digital health businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Fatima Al-Rashid describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1307,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-94f5b0c663-1024x640.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1307\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Commercial_area_of_futian_to_east2020.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Charlie fong · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Nawa Health watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Fatima Al-Rashid concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Fatima Al-Rashid finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The side project Fatima Al-Rashid could not put down','It started as a weekend fix for a problem at work. It is now a digital health business with staff.','publish','open','open','','entrepreneur-stories-ad164804','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',0,'http://localhost:8080/entrepreneur-stories-ad164804/',0,'post','',0),(150,4,'2026-05-18 23:14:24','2026-05-18 17:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Kestrel Defence Systems story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Viktor Marchenko does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko started the Warsaw company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1308,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-820bc1d1ce-1024x767.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1308\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankfurt_Skyline_2022_bei_Nacht.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in aerospace components rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Kestrel Defence Systems resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Viktor Marchenko built Kestrel Defence Systems twice. The second time worked.','What changed was not the idea. It was the sequence.','publish','open','open','','entrepreneur-stories-d60cafb9','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',0,'http://localhost:8080/entrepreneur-stories-d60cafb9/',0,'post','',0),(152,5,'2026-07-27 21:14:24','2026-07-27 15:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is unusually precise about the moment Harbourline Retail nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Harbourline Retail employs a substantial team out of Osaka and serves customers across omnichannel retail. Grace Nakamura tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1309,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-059b059d92-1024x575.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1309\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_York_City_(New_York,_USA),_Manhattan,_Skyline_--_2012_--_6696.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Harbourline Retail had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Grace Nakamura would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Harbourline Retail still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','What Grace Nakamura learned selling to the customers nobody wanted','The unfashionable segment turned out to be the one that paid on time.','publish','open','open','','entrepreneur-stories-1fca66d8','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',0,'http://localhost:8080/entrepreneur-stories-1fca66d8/',0,'post','',0),(154,6,'2026-04-11 21:14:24','2026-04-11 15:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Meridian Loop story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Priya Raghunathan does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan started the Singapore company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1310,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-1fe5eb45d1-1024x233.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1310\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyscraper_Los_Angeles_Downtown_2013.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Tuxyso · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in logistics software rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Priya Raghunathan on the pivot that saved Meridian Loop','Eighteen months of work, set aside in a single week — and the reasoning behind it.','publish','open','open','','founder-stories-b2207026','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-stories-b2207026/',0,'post','',0),(156,2,'2026-07-30 20:14:24','2026-07-30 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo is unusually precise about the moment Halden Grid nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Halden Grid employs a substantial team out of Lagos and serves customers across energy infrastructure. Daniel Okonkwo tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1311,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-cc5333e618-1024x233.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1311\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hong_Kong_skyscrapers_in_a_night_of_typhoon.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Wilfredor · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Halden Grid had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Daniel Okonkwo would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Halden Grid still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The co-founder conversation Daniel Okonkwo wishes had happened sooner','Equity, roles and exit expectations, discussed two years later than they should have been.','publish','open','open','','founder-stories-43e24724','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-stories-43e24724/',0,'post','',0),(158,3,'2026-08-18 04:14:24','2026-08-17 22:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Marta Ferreira is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Quorum Health was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable clinical operations businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Marta Ferreira describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1312,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-a7f6e6d6a8-1024x613.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1312\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%ADer_Duarte_Coelho_and_P%C3%ADer_Maur%C3%ADcio_de_Nassa_Towers_in_recife,_pernambuco,_Brazil.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Wilfredor · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Quorum Health watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Marta Ferreira concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Marta Ferreira finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Marta Ferreira: building in clinical operations when nobody believed in the category','Being early looks identical to being wrong, right up until it does not.','publish','open','open','','founder-stories-b234ac53','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-stories-b234ac53/',0,'post','',0),(160,4,'2026-07-15 20:14:24','2026-07-15 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Northgate Analytics story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Ethan Brightwell does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell started the Manchester company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1313,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-25623f9d0b-1024x613.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1313\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_the_Central_Business_District_of_Singapore_with_Esplanade_Bridge_in_the_evening.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in risk analytics rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','From first hire to two hundred: how Ethan Brightwell kept Northgate Analytics coherent','Scaling a company without scaling the confusion that usually comes with it.','publish','open','open','','founder-stories-d2c081be','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-stories-d2c081be/',0,'post','',0),(162,5,'2026-06-01 03:14:24','2026-05-31 21:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Sana Qureshi is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Tallow & Co. was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable consumer manufacturing businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Sana Qureshi describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1314,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-23dc6575c0-1024x419.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1314\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArtScience_Museum,_Marina_Bay_Sands,_Singapore.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Tallow & Co. watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Sana Qureshi concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Sana Qureshi finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Sana Qureshi took over Tallow & Co. mid-crisis. Here is the first ninety days.','No grand plan. A short list of things that had to stop immediately.','publish','open','open','','ceo-stories-0b5ce16b','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-stories-0b5ce16b/',0,'post','',0),(164,6,'2026-08-23 20:14:24','2026-08-23 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Bastion Freight story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Julien Marchetti does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti started the Marseille company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1315,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0de3464d6c-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1315\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pudong_Shanghai_November_2017_panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">King of Hearts · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in freight forwarding rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The chief executive who cut the roadmap in half','Fewer commitments, met properly, changed how customers and staff talked about Bastion Freight.','publish','open','open','','ceo-stories-ab73fe1f','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-stories-ab73fe1f/',0,'post','',0),(166,2,'2026-04-03 20:14:24','2026-04-03 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is unusually precise about the moment Kindred Payments nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Kindred Payments employs a substantial team out of Accra and serves customers across payments. Amara Boateng tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1316,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0d4f7b61eb-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1316\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seattle_(WA,_USA),_Hochh%C3%A4user_--_2022_--_1490.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Kindred Payments had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Amara Boateng would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Kindred Payments still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Amara Boateng on running Kindred Payments without a growth-at-all-costs mandate','What it looks like to answer to a board that has redefined the scoreboard.','publish','open','open','','ceo-stories-25f96bab','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-stories-25f96bab/',0,'post','',0),(168,3,'2026-04-05 01:14:24','2026-04-04 19:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Rohan Vaidya is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Sextant Labs was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable developer tooling businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Rohan Vaidya describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1024x686.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seattle_(WA,_USA),_Hochh%C3%A4user_--_2022_--_1806.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Sextant Labs watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Rohan Vaidya concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Rohan Vaidya finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Rohan Vaidya rebuilt trust after a bad year','Missed numbers, a painful restructure, and the slow work of getting the room back.','publish','open','open','','ceo-stories-8431935f','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-stories-8431935f/',0,'post','',0),(170,4,'2026-04-02 05:14:24','2026-04-01 23:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim is unusually precise about the moment Fjordline Materials nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Fjordline Materials employs a substantial team out of Bergen and serves customers across advanced materials. Ingrid Solheim tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Floating_Platform_and_illuminated_East_Coast_Parkway_seen_from_the_sky_observation_deck_of_Marina_Bay_Sands_Singapore.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Fjordline Materials had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Ingrid Solheim would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Fjordline Materials still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The management habit that separates good operators from great ones','It is not vision. It is the discipline of closing loops.','publish','open','open','','leadership-94ff31a0','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',0,'http://localhost:8080/leadership-94ff31a0/',0,'post','',0),(172,5,'2026-08-21 03:14:24','2026-08-20 21:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Caleb Whitfield is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Ardent Mutual was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable insurance businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Caleb Whitfield describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_-_ON_-_Toronto_Skyline2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Taxiarchos228 · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Ardent Mutual watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Caleb Whitfield concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Caleb Whitfield finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Caleb Whitfield on hiring for judgement, not experience','A hiring bar built around decisions people have actually had to make.','publish','open','open','','leadership-745901aa','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',0,'http://localhost:8080/leadership-745901aa/',0,'post','',0),(174,6,'2026-08-12 22:14:24','2026-08-12 16:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Orbit Assembly story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Lin Wei-Chen does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen started the Taipei company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_Financial_District_August_2017.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arild Vågen · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in industrial robotics rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Leading through ambiguity without pretending to have answers','Confidence and certainty are not the same thing, and staff can tell the difference.','publish','open','open','','leadership-27a01203','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/leadership-27a01203/',0,'post','',0),(176,2,'2026-07-30 03:14:24','2026-07-29 21:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois is unusually precise about the moment Cellar Six nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Cellar Six employs a substantial team out of Montreal and serves customers across food and beverage. Nadia Bourgeois tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_August_2017_03.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arild Vågen · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Cellar Six had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Nadia Bourgeois would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Cellar Six still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Why Nadia Bourgeois made every executive write their own strategy memo','Six pages, no slides, and a much shorter argument at the end of it.','publish','open','open','','leadership-d1810c55','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/leadership-d1810c55/',0,'post','',0),(178,3,'2026-05-25 03:14:24','2026-05-24 21:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Vesper Systems story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Tomas Novak does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak started the Prague company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1322,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-d46d8e78c5-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1322\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_(AU),_Northbank_--_2019_--_1422-7.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in building automation rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Vesper Systems spent four unglamorous years. Then everything compounded.','The overnight success took a decade, as they generally do.','publish','open','open','','success-stories-fa7cf894','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/success-stories-fa7cf894/',0,'post','',0),(180,4,'2026-07-23 23:14:24','2026-07-23 17:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour is unusually precise about the moment Rafiq Capital nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Rafiq Capital employs a substantial team out of Amman and serves customers across small-business lending. Aisha Mansour tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive, Rafiq Capital</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_(AU),_Northbank_--_2019_--_1498-502.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Rafiq Capital had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Aisha Mansour would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Rafiq Capital still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The category Rafiq Capital created by refusing to compete on price','A deliberately narrow product, sold at a premium, to customers who needed exactly it.','publish','open','open','','success-stories-4c067948','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/success-stories-4c067948/',0,'post','',0),(182,5,'2026-03-05 05:14:24','2026-03-04 23:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Gregor Lindqvist is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Baltic Yield was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable agricultural technology businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Gregor Lindqvist describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1324,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ae876be62e-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1324\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_(AU),_Northbank_--_2019_--_1509-13.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Baltic Yield watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Gregor Lindqvist concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Gregor Lindqvist finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Baltic Yield turned churn into its best growth channel','Departing customers were treated as a research programme rather than a loss.','publish','open','open','','success-stories-e4aedc6d','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/success-stories-e4aedc6d/',0,'post','',0),(184,6,'2026-08-26 22:14:24','2026-08-26 16:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Atelier Nove story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Chiara Bellandi does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi started the Milan company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1325,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-60852483f6-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1325\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_(AU),_Northbank_--_2019_--_1514-8.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in design manufacturing rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Chiara Bellandi said no to three acquisition offers. The fourth changed everything.','Holding out was not stubbornness. It was a view about where the value would sit.','publish','open','open','','success-stories-72661cc7','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/success-stories-72661cc7/',0,'post','',0),(186,2,'2026-08-22 06:14:24','2026-08-22 00:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Samuel Adeyemi where the story stops being about Longbridge Learning and starts being about workforce training itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Longbridge Learning was built in Nairobi on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Samuel Adeyemi spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1315,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0de3464d6c-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1315\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pudong_Shanghai_November_2017_panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">King of Hearts · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in workforce training depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Longbridge Learning did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Samuel Adeyemi anticipated takes another five years, Longbridge Learning has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Samuel Adeyemi is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in workforce training have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Samuel Adeyemi’s timeline is the one variable Longbridge Learning cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Samuel Adeyemi: the operator quietly rewriting workforce training','Little press, no conference circuit, and a business that keeps taking share.','trash','open','open','','founder-features-bf36950c__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-features-bf36950c/',0,'post','',0),(188,3,'2026-09-04 20:14:24','2026-09-04 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Hannah Vogel what Steinweg Logistik does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Hannah Vogel’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik operates in warehousing from Hamburg with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1316,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0d4f7b61eb-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1316\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seattle_(WA,_USA),_Hochh%C3%A4user_--_2022_--_1490.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Steinweg Logistik lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Hannah Vogel spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hannah Vogel is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Ten years, two near-deaths and one very good idea','The long version of the Steinweg Logistik story, told by the person who lived it.','trash','open','open','','founder-features-4b04b27d__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:38','2026-10-12 10:50:38','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-features-4b04b27d/',0,'post','',0),(190,4,'2026-08-16 01:14:24','2026-08-15 19:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo does not do many interviews. The Bogotá office has no press page, Ribera Foods has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Diego Salcedo laid out how Ribera Foods came to occupy the position it holds in food distribution: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1024x686.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seattle_(WA,_USA),_Hochh%C3%A4user_--_2022_--_1806.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in food distribution were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Ribera Foods did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Ribera Foods is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Ribera Foods navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Diego Salcedo is building Ribera Foods for a market that does not exist yet','A bet on where food distribution lands in five years — and a plan to survive until it does.','trash','open','open','','founder-features-e9cfa0e6__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-features-e9cfa0e6/',0,'post','',0),(192,5,'2026-04-10 02:14:24','2026-04-09 20:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Meera Krishnan where the story stops being about Aster Fieldworks and starts being about field service software itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aster Fieldworks was built in Chennai on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Meera Krishnan spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Floating_Platform_and_illuminated_East_Coast_Parkway_seen_from_the_sky_observation_deck_of_Marina_Bay_Sands_Singapore.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in field service software depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aster Fieldworks did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Meera Krishnan anticipated takes another five years, Aster Fieldworks has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Meera Krishnan is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in field service software have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Meera Krishnan’s timeline is the one variable Aster Fieldworks cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The founder who made patience a strategy','Slow hiring, slow fundraising, and a compounding rate most of the market missed.','trash','open','open','','founder-features-9b42f124__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:43','2026-10-12 10:50:43','',0,'http://localhost:8080/founder-features-9b42f124/',0,'post','',0),(194,6,'2026-06-17 22:14:24','2026-06-17 16:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth does not do many interviews. The Sydney office has no press page, Trellis Property has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Oliver Ashworth laid out how Trellis Property came to occupy the position it holds in commercial real estate: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_-_ON_-_Toronto_Skyline2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Taxiarchos228 · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in commercial real estate were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Trellis Property did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Trellis Property is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Trellis Property navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Oliver Ashworth, chief executive of Trellis Property, on scale without sprawl','Growing revenue faster than complexity is the whole job, and almost nobody does it.','trash','open','open','','ceo-features-55577b70__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-features-55577b70/',0,'post','',0),(196,2,'2026-09-03 21:14:24','2026-09-03 15:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Fatima Al-Rashid where the story stops being about Nawa Health and starts being about digital health itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health was built in Dubai on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Fatima Al-Rashid spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_Financial_District_August_2017.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arild Vågen · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in digital health depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Fatima Al-Rashid anticipated takes another five years, Nawa Health has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Fatima Al-Rashid is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in digital health have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Fatima Al-Rashid’s timeline is the one variable Nawa Health cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The chief executive who put engineers in front of customers','It broke the support process, then fixed the product.','trash','open','open','','ceo-features-7b529d13__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:38','2026-10-12 10:50:38','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-features-7b529d13/',0,'post','',0),(198,3,'2026-04-29 02:14:24','2026-04-28 20:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Viktor Marchenko what Kestrel Defence Systems does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Viktor Marchenko’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Kestrel Defence Systems operates in aerospace components from Warsaw with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toronto_August_2017_03.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arild Vågen · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Kestrel Defence Systems lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Viktor Marchenko spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Viktor Marchenko: “The job is choosing what not to do.”','A long conversation about focus, capital allocation and saying no in public.','trash','open','open','','ceo-features-2989cdcb__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:42','2026-10-12 10:50:42','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-features-2989cdcb/',0,'post','',0),(200,4,'2026-03-21 06:14:24','2026-03-21 00:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura does not do many interviews. The Osaka office has no press page, Harbourline Retail has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Grace Nakamura laid out how Harbourline Retail came to occupy the position it holds in omnichannel retail: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1322,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-d46d8e78c5-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1322\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_(AU),_Northbank_--_2019_--_1422-7.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in omnichannel retail were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Harbourline Retail did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Harbourline Retail is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Harbourline Retail navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside Grace Nakamura’s three-year plan for Harbourline Retail','Three priorities, one number each, and an unusual amount of specificity.','trash','open','open','','ceo-features-82c34945__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:44','2026-10-12 10:50:44','',0,'http://localhost:8080/ceo-features-82c34945/',0,'post','',0),(202,5,'2026-07-18 20:14:24','2026-07-18 14:44:24','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Priya Raghunathan what Meridian Loop does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Priya Raghunathan’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop operates in logistics software from Singapore with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1024x768.jpg\" alt=\"City skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_(AU),_Northbank_--_2019_--_1498-502.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dietmar Rabich · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Meridian Loop lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Priya Raghunathan spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The quiet reinvention of Meridian Loop','No rebrand, no relaunch — just a different business at the end of it.','trash','open','open','','business-features-0b1bcec6__trashed','','','2026-10-12 16:20:40','2026-10-12 10:50:40','',0,'http://localhost:8080/business-features-0b1bcec6/',0,'post','',0),(204,6,'2026-03-27 06:14:24