','open','closed','','dispositif-et-vehicule-de-transmission-en-directe-de-la-celebration-du-64e-anniversaire-de-lindependance-du-benin-en-2024-04-cropped','','','2026-10-12 16:46:58','2026-10-12 11:16:58','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-f3acfaeb97.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1390,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:08','2026-09-08 04:58:08','Media, broadcasting and publishing. Photo: Jochen Teufel (CC BY-SA 3.0), via Wikimedia Commons.','Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung Aushang Fußgängerzone (2009)','','inherit','open','closed','','neue-osnabrucker-zeitung-aushang-fusgangerzone-2009','','','2026-10-12 16:46:59','2026-10-12 11:16:59','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-4fc53455ed.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1391,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:09','2026-09-08 04:58:09','Media, broadcasting and publishing. Photo: Victor Vizu (CC BY 3.0), via Wikimedia Commons.','Soiuzpechat Kharkov 1','','inherit','open','closed','','soiuzpechat-kharkov-1','','','2026-10-12 16:47:00','2026-10-12 11:17:00','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-f994194768.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1392,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:11','2026-09-08 04:58:11','Media, broadcasting and publishing. Photo: Taxiarchos228 (FAL), via Wikimedia Commons.','Basel - Zeitungsständer','','inherit','open','closed','','basel-zeitungsstander','','','2026-10-12 16:47:02','2026-10-12 11:17:02','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-1a73a461cc.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1393,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:13','2026-09-08 04:58:13','Media, broadcasting and publishing. Photo: Dietmar Rabich (CC BY-SA 4.0), via Wikimedia Commons.','San Diego (California, USA), San Diego Avenue, Zeitungsautomaten -- 2012 -- 5329','','inherit','open','closed','','san-diego-california-usa-san-diego-avenue-zeitungsautomaten-2012-5329','','','2026-10-12 16:47:04','2026-10-12 11:17:04','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-7a38ed2796.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1394,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:15','2026-09-08 04:58:15','Media, broadcasting and publishing. Photo: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas (CC BY-SA 3.0), via Wikimedia Commons.','KotaKinabalu Radio-KKFM-Kota-Kinabalu-01','','inherit','open','closed','','kotakinabalu-radio-kkfm-kota-kinabalu-01','','','2026-10-12 16:47:05','2026-10-12 11:17:05','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-8fc8b6c927.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1395,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:17','2026-09-08 04:58:17','Media, broadcasting and publishing. Photo: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas (CC BY-SA 3.0), via Wikimedia Commons.','KotaKinabalu Radio-KKFM-Kota-Kinabalu-02','','inherit','open','closed','','kotakinabalu-radio-kkfm-kota-kinabalu-02','','','2026-10-12 16:47:06','2026-10-12 11:17:06','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-7a751901ed.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1396,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in logistics software was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop is one of them. Founded in Singapore and run by Priya Raghunathan, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1335,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-bf7da0674c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1335\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Priya Raghunathan is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Meridian Loop is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Meridian Loop posts a record quarter as logistics software demand holds','Renewals carried the number, but management flagged a tighter margin path into next year.','inherit','closed','closed','','106-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','',106,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1396',0,'revision','',0),(1397,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Halden Grid reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a energy infrastructure market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo, founder at the Lagos-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Halden Grid does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1361,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-f1aa6e1086-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1361\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:International_Container_Transshipment_Terminal,_Kochi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">BgbwikiV4 · CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For energy infrastructure businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Halden Grid’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Daniel Okonkwo is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Halden Grid is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Mid-market consolidation accelerates as Halden Grid closes its third deal','The acquisition is small. The pattern behind it is not.','inherit','closed','closed','','108-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','',108,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1397',0,'revision','',0),(1398,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across clinical operations and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Quorum Health, Marta Ferreira moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1362,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-b31965eba4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1362\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kochi_Container_Terminal_-_%E0%B4%95%E0%B5%8A%E0%B4%9A%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%9A%E0%B4%BF_%E0%B4%95%E0%B4%A3%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%9F%E0%B5%88%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%BC_%E0%B4%9F%E0%B5%86%E0%B5%BC%E0%B4%AE%E0%B4%BF%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%BD.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Shijan Kaakkara · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in clinical operations are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in clinical operations, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Boards are still budgeting for shocks that have not arrived','Supply chains have steadied. Contingency lines in the budget have not come down.','inherit','closed','closed','','110-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:31','2026-09-08 04:58:31','',110,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1398',0,'revision','',0),(1399,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in risk analytics was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics is one of them. Founded in Manchester and run by Ethan Brightwell, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1363,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-ebd4a0a38e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1363\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mogral_at_the_International_Container_Transshipment_Terminal,_Kochi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">ArulkumarRajamani · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Ethan Brightwell is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Northgate Analytics is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Northgate Analytics takes its risk analytics platform into three new markets','A deliberate, unglamorous expansion — and a test of whether the model travels.','inherit','closed','closed','','112-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',112,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1399',0,'revision','',0),(1400,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across consumer manufacturing and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Tallow & Co., Sana Qureshi moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1364,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-795c40de59-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1364\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kochi_container_terminal.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Challiyan at Malayalam Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in consumer manufacturing are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in consumer manufacturing, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The quiet return of operating discipline','After a decade of growth-first orthodoxy, the companies setting the pace are the ones counting carefully.','inherit','closed','closed','','114-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',114,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1400',0,'revision','',0),(1401,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in freight forwarding was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight is one of them. Founded in Marseille and run by Julien Marchetti, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1365,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-fd8ecda3dc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1365\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_in_vallarpadam_container_terminal_kochi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Calvin joseph · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Julien Marchetti is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Bastion Freight is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Why freight forwarding buyers are consolidating vendors','Procurement is shrinking supplier lists. Challengers are discovering what that costs them.','inherit','closed','closed','','116-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',116,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1401',0,'revision','',0),(1402,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Kindred Payments reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a payments market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng, chief executive at the Accra-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Kindred Payments does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1366,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-fdda5f1f19-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1366\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_in_vallarpadam_container_terminal_kochi_02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Calvin joseph · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For payments businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Kindred Payments’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Amara Boateng is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Kindred Payments is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Hybrid work settled. Now companies are redesigning around it.','The argument is over. The expensive part — rebuilding how work actually happens — is starting.','inherit','closed','closed','','118-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',118,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1402',0,'revision','',0),(1403,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across developer tooling and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Sextant Labs, Rohan Vaidya moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1367,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-05ceec19b2-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1367\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vallarpadam_Container_Terminal.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Bharat S Raj · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in developer tooling are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in developer tooling, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Capital is patient again, and it is changing how companies plan','Longer horizons are producing different decisions, and a different kind of company.','inherit','closed','closed','','120-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:32','2026-09-08 04:58:32','',120,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1403',0,'revision','',0),(1404,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Fjordline Materials reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a advanced materials market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim, founder at the Bergen-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Fjordline Materials does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1368,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-1087464baf-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1368\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vallarpadam_Container_Terminal_at_night.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ashlyak · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For advanced materials businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Fjordline Materials’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Ingrid Solheim is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Fjordline Materials is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside Fjordline Materials: the operating model behind five years of compounding','No single breakthrough. A hundred decisions that pointed the same direction.','inherit','closed','closed','','122-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',122,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1404',0,'revision','',0),(1405,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across insurance and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Ardent Mutual, Caleb Whitfield moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1369,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-33efe4654e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1369\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cochin_International_Contrainer_Transhipment_Terminal.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Dr. Ajay Balachandran · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in insurance are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in insurance, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Ardent Mutual rebuilt its insurance business without losing customers','A replatforming that could have gone badly, executed in public, over three years.','inherit','closed','closed','','124-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',124,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1405',0,'revision','',0),(1406,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in industrial robotics was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly is one of them. Founded in Taipei and run by Lin Wei-Chen, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1370,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-81596e3387-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1370\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vallapadam_Container_Terminal_view_from_Fort_Cochin_-_panoramio_(3).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Jaseem Hamza · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Lin Wei-Chen is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Orbit Assembly is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Orbit Assembly bets its next decade on services, not licences','The margin profile changes. So does almost everything else about the company.','inherit','closed','closed','','126-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',126,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1406',0,'revision','',0),(1407,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Cellar Six reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a food and beverage market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois, founder at the Montreal-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Cellar Six does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1371,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-43d2a1fa0a-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1371\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vallarpadam_Container_terminal_at_night_IMG_20260505_191934770.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ranjithsiji · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For food and beverage businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Cellar Six’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Nadia Bourgeois is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Cellar Six is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The org chart that let Cellar Six double without doubling headcount','Structure as strategy: what Nadia Bourgeois changed, and what it cost.','inherit','closed','closed','','128-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',128,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1407',0,'revision','',0),(1408,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in building automation was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems is one of them. Founded in Prague and run by Tomas Novak, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1372,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-6441a69292-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1372\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12049_(UBL)_WDG-4_loco_with_a_freight_train.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Adityamadhav83 · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Tomas Novak is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Vesper Systems is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Boards are asking harder questions about AI. Vesper Systems has answers.','Governance has caught up with the technology faster than most executives expected.','inherit','closed','closed','','130-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',130,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1408',0,'revision','',0),(1409,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-0