ph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For small-business lending businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Rafiq Capital’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Aisha Mansour is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Rafiq Capital is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Governance is no longer a compliance line item','The companies treating it as strategy are getting cheaper capital and calmer boards.','inherit','closed','closed','','132-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:33','2026-09-08 04:58:33','',132,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1409',0,'revision','',0),(1410,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across agricultural technology and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Baltic Yield, Gregor Lindqvist moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1374,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-5b5f2c9db5-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1374\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Railways_BCNHL_freight_wagon.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ravi Raj · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in agricultural technology are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in agricultural technology, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','What Baltic Yield’s succession plan says about modern leadership','Announced early, rehearsed properly, and unusually specific about what happens next.','inherit','closed','closed','','134-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',134,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1410',0,'revision','',0),(1411,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in design manufacturing was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove is one of them. Founded in Milan and run by Chiara Bellandi, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1375,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-e78c424d9e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1375\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VSKP_based_Twin_WDG3A_Locomotives_with_a_freight_train_02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Adityamadhav83 · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Chiara Bellandi is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Atelier Nove is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside the restructure Chiara Bellandi spent two years arguing for','A reorganisation nobody wanted, that most of the executive team now defends.','inherit','closed','closed','','136-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',136,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1411',0,'revision','',0),(1412,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across workforce training and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Longbridge Learning, Samuel Adeyemi moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1376,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-f8ebfc8c96-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1376\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WAG_9_Locomotive_with_Northbound_freight_at_Pendurthi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Adityamadhav83 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in workforce training are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in workforce training, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Workforce training, mapped: where the margin is moving','Value is shifting down the stack. The companies that noticed first are already repositioning.','inherit','closed','closed','','138-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',138,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1412',0,'revision','',0),(1413,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For most of the past decade, the safest assumption in warehousing was that scale would eventually solve the margin problem. That assumption is now being tested by a cohort of mid-sized companies that never got large enough to benefit from it and have had to find another route.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik is one of them. Founded in Hamburg and run by Hannah Vogel, it competes against businesses several times its size and has spent the last two years deliberately declining the deals that would have made it look like them. Its win rate in competitive procurement has roughly doubled over the same period.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Every large deal we lost taught us something. Every large deal we won badly cost us a year.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1377,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-a63a57e88e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1377\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Scale brings complexity long before it brings margin. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WDG4D_Freight_Locomotive.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Apurba Biswas · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The economics beneath the growth rate</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are not mysterious. Servicing very large customers requires implementation capacity, compliance work and account management that mid-sized companies fund out of margin they do not have. The revenue arrives, the cost arrives sooner, and the business spends two years growing into an obligation it took on in an afternoon.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik’s response was to define, in writing, what it would not sell. Contracts requiring bespoke integration work were declined outright. Deals below a floor price were routed to a partner. The immediate effect was a slower pipeline; the second-order effect was a support organisation that stopped being the constraint on everything else.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What this means for the sector</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-market companies are increasingly competing on fit rather than feature parity.</li>\n<li>Declining business is becoming a documented policy rather than a case-by-case judgement.</li>\n<li>The cost of a badly-scoped enterprise contract now shows up in retention, not just margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether the discipline survives a genuinely bad year is the open question. Hannah Vogel is candid that the policy has never been tested against a missed number. For now, though, Steinweg Logistik is a useful counter-example to the idea that the only route through a difficult market is to take whatever revenue is offered.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Five forces reshaping warehousing — and who is positioned for them','A sector read from the desks that cover it closest.','inherit','closed','closed','','140-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',140,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1413',0,'revision','',0),(1414,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The numbers Ribera Foods reported this quarter were solid enough to be unremarkable, which is precisely what makes them worth reading. In a food distribution market where the loudest companies have spent two years explaining variance, a business that simply did what it said it would do is now a minority position.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive at the Bogotá-based company, has spent three years narrowing what Ribera Foods does. Product lines were cut, two geographies were exited, and a services arm that had grown by accretion was folded back into the core. The result is a company smaller in scope than it was three years ago and materially more profitable.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were confusing motion with progress. Once you stop doing the things that don’t compound, the business gets legible very quickly.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1378,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-f5202dc109-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1378\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The business district where much of this reporting was done. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WDM-3A_locomotive_of_Indian_Railways.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ravi Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the market is actually rewarding</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Investors and enterprise buyers have converged on a similar test. Both want evidence that a company can absorb a bad quarter without a restructure. That has shifted the premium away from growth rate alone and towards the durability of the revenue underneath it — renewal rates, contract length, and how much of the base would survive a procurement review.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For food distribution businesses in particular, that has meant re-examining a decade of assumptions. Land-and-expand models built for cheap capital look different when the expansion motion has to fund itself. Several of Ribera Foods’s closest competitors have responded by discounting into multi-year commitments, a move that protects the reported number while quietly worsening the economics.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Revenue quality now carries more weight than revenue growth in almost every board conversation.</li>\n<li>Exiting a market reads as a strategic act rather than an admission of failure — and is being priced that way.</li>\n<li>Discounting into longer contracts protects headline metrics while damaging the business underneath them.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>None of this is a dramatic story, and Diego Salcedo is uninterested in making it one. But the companies emerging from this period in the strongest position are, almost without exception, the ones that spent it doing fewer things properly. Ribera Foods is a reasonable case study in what that looks like from the inside.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The unglamorous infrastructure quietly deciding food distribution’s winners','Nobody puts it in a pitch deck. It determines who can serve the biggest customers.','inherit','closed','closed','','142-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',142,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1414',0,'revision','',0),(1415,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Talk to enough executives across field service software and a pattern emerges that does not show up in any of the sector reports: companies are quietly rebuilding their planning processes around longer horizons, and it is changing what they choose to build.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At Aster Fieldworks, Meera Krishnan moved the business from an annual plan with quarterly revisions to a three-year capital allocation framework reviewed twice a year. The change sounds procedural. Its effect on the product roadmap has been anything but.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you plan in twelve-month blocks, you only ever fund things that pay back inside twelve months. That is not a strategy, it is a constraint pretending to be one.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1379,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-industry-ec82f9b63c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Industry, transport and infrastructure in India\" class=\"wp-image-1379\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Operating decisions rarely look dramatic from the outside. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Twin_WDG4_Locos_with_a_Tanker_freight_at_Medchal.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Adityamadhav83 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why planning horizons changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Two forces are behind the shift. The first is the cost of capital, which has made the option value of waiting genuinely expensive and forced companies to commit earlier. The second is customer behaviour: enterprise buyers in field service software are signing longer agreements and expect their suppliers to still exist, in a recognisable form, at the end of them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The practical consequence is a different kind of investment. Infrastructure work that would previously have lost every prioritisation argument is now getting funded, because the payback period no longer disqualifies it. Several companies report that the first year of the new framework was mostly spent paying down debt accumulated under the old one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Planning horizon is a strategic choice, and most companies inherit theirs by accident.</li>\n<li>Longer horizons make previously unfundable infrastructure work viable.</li>\n<li>Customers increasingly evaluate supplier durability as part of procurement, not after it.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is early to say whether the discipline holds through a downturn. But the companies that made the change first are already describing a different set of options than those still planning twelve months at a time — and in field service software, that gap tends to widen rather than close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Why field service software incumbents keep losing the middle of the market','Too expensive for the bottom, too rigid for the top. The squeeze is structural.','inherit','closed','closed','','144-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',144,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1415',0,'revision','',0),(1416,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is unusually precise about the moment Trellis Property nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Trellis Property employs a substantial team out of Sydney and serves customers across commercial real estate. Oliver Ashworth tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1306,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8336172513-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1306\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_Worli_Sea-Link_(cropped).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">yatriksheth · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Trellis Property had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Oliver Ashworth would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Trellis Property still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Oliver Ashworth: “We were nine months from closing. Then one customer said yes.”','The story of Trellis Property, told from the point where it very nearly did not happen.','inherit','closed','closed','','146-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:34','2026-09-08 04:58:34','',146,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1416',0,'revision','',0),(1417,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Fatima Al-Rashid is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Nawa Health was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable digital health businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Fatima Al-Rashid describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1307,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-94f5b0c663-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1307\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_03-2016_80_Dadar_Beach_view_of_the_SeaLink.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">A.Savin · FAL</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Nawa Health watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Fatima Al-Rashid concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Fatima Al-Rashid finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The side project Fatima Al-Rashid could not put down','It started as a weekend fix for a problem at work. It is now a digital health business with staff.','inherit','closed','closed','','148-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',148,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1417',0,'revision','',0),(1418,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Kestrel Defence Systems story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Viktor Marchenko does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko started the Warsaw company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1308,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-820bc1d1ce-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1308\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_Bandra-Worli_Sea_Link.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Rutiknatekar · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in aerospace components rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Kestrel Defence Systems resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Viktor Marchenko built Kestrel Defence Systems twice. The second time worked.','What changed was not the idea. It was the sequence.','inherit','closed','closed','','150-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',150,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1418',0,'revision','',0),(1419,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is unusually precise about the moment Harbourline Retail nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Harbourline Retail employs a substantial team out of Osaka and serves customers across omnichannel retail. Grace Nakamura tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1309,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-059b059d92-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1309\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_worli_sealink%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%A6%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B0-%E0%B4%B5%E0%B5%BC%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%BF_%E0%B4%95%E0%B4%9F%E0%B5%BD%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B2%E0%B4%82.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arjuncm3 · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Harbourline Retail had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Grace Nakamura would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Harbourline Retail still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','What Grace Nakamura learned selling to the customers nobody wanted','The unfashionable segment turned out to be the one that paid on time.','inherit','closed','closed','','152-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',152,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1419',0,'revision','',0),(1420,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Meridian Loop story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Priya Raghunathan does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan started the Singapore company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1310,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-1fe5eb45d1-1200x437.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1310\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangalore_Panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Muhammad Mahdi Karim · GFDL 1.2</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in logistics software rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Priya Raghunathan on the pivot that saved Meridian Loop','Eighteen months of work, set aside in a single week — and the reasoning behind it.','inherit','closed','closed','','154-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',154,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1420',0,'revision','',0),(1421,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo is unusually precise about the moment Halden Grid nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Halden Grid employs a substantial team out of Lagos and serves customers across energy infrastructure. Daniel Okonkwo tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1311,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-cc5333e618-1200x437.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1311\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangalore_Panorama_edit1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Muhammad Mahdi Karim · GFDL 1.2</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Halden Grid had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Daniel Okonkwo would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Halden Grid still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The co-founder conversation Daniel Okonkwo wishes had happened sooner','Equity, roles and exit expectations, discussed two years later than they should have been.','inherit','closed','closed','','156-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',156,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1421',0,'revision','',0),(1422,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Marta Ferreira is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Quorum Health was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable clinical operations businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Marta Ferreira describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1312,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-a7f6e6d6a8-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1312\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UB_City_(5392886532).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Saad Faruque from Bangalore, India · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Quorum Health watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Marta Ferreira concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Marta Ferreira finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Marta Ferreira: building in clinical operations when nobody believed in the category','Being early looks identical to being wrong, right up until it does not.','inherit','closed','closed','','158-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:35','2026-09-08 04:58:35','',158,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1422',0,'revision','',0),(1423,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Northgate Analytics story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Ethan Brightwell does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell started the Manchester company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1313,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-25623f9d0b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1313\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_Worli_sea_link,_Mumbai.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Rahulransubhe · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in risk analytics rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','From first hire to two hundred: how Ethan Brightwell kept Northgate Analytics coherent','Scaling a company without scaling the confusion that usually comes with it.','inherit','closed','closed','','160-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',160,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1423',0,'revision','',0),(1424,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Sana Qureshi is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Tallow & Co. was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable consumer manufacturing businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Sana Qureshi describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1314,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-23dc6575c0-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1314\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_Worli_Sea_Link_from_worli_Sea_Face.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Roshan Jain · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Tallow & Co. watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Sana Qureshi concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Sana Qureshi finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Sana Qureshi took over Tallow & Co. mid-crisis. Here is the first ninety days.','No grand plan. A short list of things that had to stop immediately.','inherit','closed','closed','','162-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',162,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1424',0,'revision','',0),(1425,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Bastion Freight story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Julien Marchetti does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti started the Marseille company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1315,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0de3464d6c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1315\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_B_Block_at_night,_Delhi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in freight forwarding rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The chief executive who cut the roadmap in half','Fewer commitments, met properly, changed how customers and staff talked about Bastion Freight.','inherit','closed','closed','','164-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',164,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1425',0,'revision','',0),(1426,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is unusually precise about the moment Kindred Payments nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Kindred Payments employs a substantial team out of Accra and serves customers across payments. Amara Boateng tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1316,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0d4f7b61eb-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1316\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_New_Delhi_at_night.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Kindred Payments had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Amara Boateng would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Kindred Payments still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Amara Boateng on running Kindred Payments without a growth-at-all-costs mandate','What it looks like to answer to a board that has redefined the scoreboard.','inherit','closed','closed','','166-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',166,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1426',0,'revision','',0),(1427,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Rohan Vaidya is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Sextant Labs was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable developer tooling businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Rohan Vaidya describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place_sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ville Miettinen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Sextant Labs watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Rohan Vaidya concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Rohan Vaidya finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Rohan Vaidya rebuilt trust after a bad year','Missed numbers, a painful restructure, and the slow work of getting the room back.','inherit','closed','closed','','168-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',168,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1427',0,'revision','',0),(1428,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim is unusually precise about the moment Fjordline Materials nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Fjordline Materials employs a substantial team out of Bergen and serves customers across advanced materials. Ingrid Solheim tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(26528).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Fjordline Materials had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Ingrid Solheim would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Fjordline Materials still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The management habit that separates good operators from great ones','It is not vision. It is the discipline of closing loops.','inherit','closed','closed','','170-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',170,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1428',0,'revision','',0),(1429,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Caleb Whitfield is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Ardent Mutual was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable insurance businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Caleb Whitfield describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(37550).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Ardent Mutual watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Caleb Whitfield concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Caleb Whitfield finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Caleb Whitfield on hiring for judgement, not experience','A hiring bar built around decisions people have actually had to make.','inherit','closed','closed','','172-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:36','2026-09-08 04:58:36','',172,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1429',0,'revision','',0),(1430,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Orbit Assembly story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Lin Wei-Chen does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen started the Taipei company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(47183).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in industrial robotics rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Leading through ambiguity without pretending to have answers','Confidence and certainty are not the same thing, and staff can tell the difference.','inherit','closed','closed','','174-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',174,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1430',0,'revision','',0),(1431,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois is unusually precise about the moment Cellar Six nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Cellar Six employs a substantial team out of Montreal and serves customers across food and beverage. Nadia Bourgeois tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(80844).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Cellar Six had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Nadia Bourgeois would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Cellar Six still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Why Nadia Bourgeois made every executive write their own strategy memo','Six pages, no slides, and a much shorter argument at the end of it.','inherit','closed','closed','','176-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',176,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1431',0,'revision','',0),(1432,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Vesper Systems story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Tomas Novak does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak started the Prague company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1322,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-d46d8e78c5-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1322\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City_buildings_(30087).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in building automation rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Vesper Systems spent four unglamorous years. Then everything compounded.','The overnight success took a decade, as they generally do.','inherit','closed','closed','','178-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',178,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1432',0,'revision','',0),(1433,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour is unusually precise about the moment Rafiq Capital nearly ended. It was a Tuesday, there were eleven weeks of runway left, and the plan on the whiteboard involved selling the company to a competitor for slightly less than it owed its suppliers.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That was four years ago. Today Rafiq Capital employs a substantial team out of Amman and serves customers across small-business lending. Aisha Mansour tells the story without any of the redemption-arc framing that usually accompanies it, which is what makes it useful.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Nobody tells you that the worst part isn’t the fear. It’s having to keep making ordinary decisions well while you are that frightened.</p><cite>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive, Rafiq Capital</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Leadership is mostly a series of ordinary decisions, made well. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(98608).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What actually changed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The turn did not come from a pivot, a new investor or a redesign. It came from a decision to stop selling to the segment Rafiq Capital had been chasing — larger organisations with long procurement cycles and no urgency — and to concentrate entirely on a smaller, less prestigious group of buyers who had the same problem and needed it solved that quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revenue per customer fell. Sales cycle length fell further. Within two quarters the business was covering its own costs, and within a year it had the credibility, and the reference customers, to go back upmarket on much better terms.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What Aisha Mansour would tell an earlier version of themselves</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Urgency in the customer matters more than budget in the customer.</li>\n<li>A smaller market you can actually win beats a larger one you are theoretically eligible for.</li>\n<li>The decision that saves the company is usually one that was available months earlier.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour is now on the other side of the problem, worrying about organisational design and succession rather than payroll. But the discipline from that period has stayed: Rafiq Capital still reviews its customer segmentation every quarter, and still writes down, explicitly, who it is not for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The category Rafiq Capital created by refusing to compete on price','A deliberately narrow product, sold at a premium, to customers who needed exactly it.','inherit','closed','closed','','180-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',180,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1433',0,'revision','',0),(1434,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most interesting thing about Gregor Lindqvist is not the company. It is the deliberate, almost stubborn slowness with which Baltic Yield was built — a pace that looked like a weakness for four years and now looks like the entire advantage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield took outside capital late, hired roughly a third as fast as comparable agricultural technology businesses, and turned down two acquisition approaches. Gregor Lindqvist describes none of this as principled. It was, they say, mostly a series of decisions that seemed obvious at the time and unusual only in aggregate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We were never trying to be patient. We just could not see a reason to do any of it faster.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1324,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ae876be62e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1324\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Most founder stories are built in rooms like this one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemon_Tree_hotel_in_HITEC_city.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What slow buys you</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hiring slowly produced a team where nearly everyone had been through a full customer lifecycle. Raising late meant the round was priced against real retention data rather than a projection. Neither is a strategy in itself, but together they produced a company with unusually low variance — a business that behaves the same way in a good quarter and a bad one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The cost was real. Baltic Yield watched two competitors take share in adjacent segments it could plausibly have won, and Gregor Lindqvist concedes that one of those markets is now closed. The trade was made knowingly: breadth was exchanged for a depth of customer understanding that has proved much harder for later entrants to replicate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Speed is a choice with a price, and the price is usually paid later.</li>\n<li>Hiring pace determines how much institutional knowledge survives the first difficult year.</li>\n<li>Raising against evidence rather than projection changes who ends up on the cap table.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield is now growing faster than at any point in its history, which Gregor Lindqvist finds mildly amusing. The company that spent four years refusing to hurry is, on the current trajectory, the one best equipped to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Baltic Yield turned churn into its best growth channel','Departing customers were treated as a research programme rather than a loss.','inherit','closed','closed','','182-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',182,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1434',0,'revision','',0),(1435,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a version of the Atelier Nove story that fits neatly into a conference keynote. Chiara Bellandi does not tell that version. The real one involves a co-founder split, eighteen months of work discarded in a week, and a business that only became coherent once its founders admitted they had been solving two different problems.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi started the Milan company with a technical co-founder while both were still employed elsewhere. The product worked. The disagreement was never about the product — it was about who it was for, and neither had ever said so directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We agreed on everything except the customer. It turns out that is the only thing you actually need to agree on.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1325,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-60852483f6-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1325\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The unglamorous middle years, where companies are actually made. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(96390).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The conversation that should have happened first</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Founder disputes in design manufacturing rarely present as disputes. They present as a roadmap that keeps splitting, a sales process that cannot be described consistently, and a team that gets different answers depending on which founder they ask. By the time it becomes explicit, a great deal of work has usually been done in two directions.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove resolved it the hard way: a written strategy memo from each founder, circulated to the other, followed by a genuinely difficult week. One version won. The other founder stayed, with a clearly defined remit, and the company shipped more in the following six months than in the preceding eighteen.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Founder alignment is about the customer, not the product or the equity split.</li>\n<li>Writing the strategy down forces disagreements into the open where they can be resolved.</li>\n<li>Ambiguity at the top reliably becomes confusion three layers down.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi now insists on the same exercise annually, with the executive team rather than the founders. It is, by their own account, the least popular meeting in the company and the one they would protect first.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Chiara Bellandi said no to three acquisition offers. The fourth changed everything.','Holding out was not stubbornness. It was a view about where the value would sit.','inherit','closed','closed','','184-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:37','2026-09-08 04:58:37','',184,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1435',0,'revision','',0),(1436,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Samuel Adeyemi where the story stops being about Longbridge Learning and starts being about workforce training itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Longbridge Learning was built in Nairobi on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Samuel Adeyemi spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1315,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0de3464d6c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1315\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_B_Block_at_night,_Delhi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in workforce training depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Longbridge Learning did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Samuel Adeyemi anticipated takes another five years, Longbridge Learning has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Samuel Adeyemi is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in workforce training have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Samuel Adeyemi’s timeline is the one variable Longbridge Learning cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Samuel Adeyemi: the operator quietly rewriting workforce training','Little press, no conference circuit, and a business that keeps taking share.','inherit','closed','closed','','186-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','',186,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1436',0,'revision','',0),(1437,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Hannah Vogel what Steinweg Logistik does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Hannah Vogel’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik operates in warehousing from Hamburg with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1316,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0d4f7b61eb-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1316\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_New_Delhi_at_night.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Steinweg Logistik lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Hannah Vogel spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hannah Vogel is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Ten years, two near-deaths and one very good idea','The long version of the Steinweg Logistik story, told by the person who lived it.','inherit','closed','closed','','188-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','',188,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1437',0,'revision','',0),(1438,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo does not do many interviews. The Bogotá office has no press page, Ribera Foods has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Diego Salcedo laid out how Ribera Foods came to occupy the position it holds in food distribution: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place_sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ville Miettinen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in food distribution were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Ribera Foods did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Ribera Foods is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Ribera Foods navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Diego Salcedo is building Ribera Foods for a market that does not exist yet','A bet on where food distribution lands in five years — and a plan to survive until it does.','inherit','closed','closed','','190-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','',190,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1438',0,'revision','',0),(1439,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Meera Krishnan where the story stops being about Aster Fieldworks and starts being about field service software itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aster Fieldworks was built in Chennai on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Meera Krishnan spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(26528).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in field service software depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aster Fieldworks did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Meera Krishnan anticipated takes another five years, Aster Fieldworks has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Meera Krishnan is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in field service software have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Meera Krishnan’s timeline is the one variable Aster Fieldworks cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The founder who made patience a strategy','Slow hiring, slow fundraising, and a compounding rate most of the market missed.','inherit','closed','closed','','192-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','',192,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1439',0,'revision','',0),(1440,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth does not do many interviews. The Sydney office has no press page, Trellis Property has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Oliver Ashworth laid out how Trellis Property came to occupy the position it holds in commercial real estate: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(37550).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in commercial real estate were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Trellis Property did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Trellis Property is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Trellis Property navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Oliver Ashworth, chief executive of Trellis Property, on scale without sprawl','Growing revenue faster than complexity is the whole job, and almost nobody does it.','inherit','closed','closed','','194-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','',194,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1440',0,'revision','',0),(1441,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Fatima Al-Rashid where the story stops being about Nawa Health and starts being about digital health itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health was built in Dubai on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Fatima Al-Rashid spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(47183).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in digital health depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Fatima Al-Rashid anticipated takes another five years, Nawa Health has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Fatima Al-Rashid is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in digital health have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Fatima Al-Rashid’s timeline is the one variable Nawa Health cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The chief executive who put engineers in front of customers','It broke the support process, then fixed the product.','inherit','closed','closed','','196-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','',196,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1441',0,'revision','',0),(1442,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Viktor Marchenko what Kestrel Defence Systems does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Viktor Marchenko’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Kestrel Defence Systems operates in aerospace components from Warsaw with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(80844).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Kestrel Defence Systems lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Viktor Marchenko spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Viktor Marchenko: “The job is choosing what not to do.”','A long conversation about focus, capital allocation and saying no in public.','inherit','closed','closed','','198-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:38','2026-09-08 04:58:38','',198,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1442',0,'revision','',0),(1443,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura does not do many interviews. The Osaka office has no press page, Harbourline Retail has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Grace Nakamura laid out how Harbourline Retail came to occupy the position it holds in omnichannel retail: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1322,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-d46d8e78c5-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1322\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City_buildings_(30087).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in omnichannel retail were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Harbourline Retail did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Harbourline Retail is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Harbourline Retail navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside Grace Nakamura’s three-year plan for Harbourline Retail','Three priorities, one number each, and an unusual amount of specificity.','inherit','closed','closed','','200-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','',200,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1443',0,'revision','',0),(1444,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Priya Raghunathan what Meridian Loop does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Priya Raghunathan’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop operates in logistics software from Singapore with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(98608).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Meridian Loop lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Priya Raghunathan spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The quiet reinvention of Meridian Loop','No rebrand, no relaunch — just a different business at the end of it.','inherit','closed','closed','','202-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','',202,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1444',0,'revision','',0),(1445,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo does not do many interviews. The Lagos office has no press page, Halden Grid has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Daniel Okonkwo laid out how Halden Grid came to occupy the position it holds in energy infrastructure: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1324,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ae876be62e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1324\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemon_Tree_hotel_in_HITEC_city.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in energy infrastructure were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Halden Grid did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Halden Grid is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Halden Grid navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How a energy infrastructure company became an infrastructure business','The product other companies build on top of, and the margin that comes with it.','inherit','closed','closed','','204-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','',204,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1445',0,'revision','',0),(1446,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Marta Ferreira where the story stops being about Quorum Health and starts being about clinical operations itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health was built in Lisbon on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Marta Ferreira spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1325,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-60852483f6-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1325\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(96390).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in clinical operations depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Marta Ferreira anticipated takes another five years, Quorum Health has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Marta Ferreira is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in clinical operations have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Marta Ferreira’s timeline is the one variable Quorum Health cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Quorum Health’s bet on doing less, better','Half the product line, twice the attachment rate, and a much simpler company.','inherit','closed','closed','','206-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','',206,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1446',0,'revision','',0),(1447,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Ethan Brightwell what Northgate Analytics does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Ethan Brightwell’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics operates in risk analytics from Manchester with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1326,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-7d34418678-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1326\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Central_business_district%27s_Skyline.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Northgate Analytics lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Ethan Brightwell spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Anatomy of a turnaround: Northgate Analytics, four years on','What actually worked, what was luck, and what the management team would repeat.','inherit','closed','closed','','208-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','',208,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1447',0,'revision','',0),(1448,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Sana Qureshi where the story stops being about Tallow & Co. and starts being about consumer manufacturing itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. was built in Karachi on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Sana Qureshi spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1327,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-61ae1687ee-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1327\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti_2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in consumer manufacturing depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Sana Qureshi anticipated takes another five years, Tallow & Co. has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Sana Qureshi is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in consumer manufacturing have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Sana Qureshi’s timeline is the one variable Tallow & Co. cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Tallow & Co. is small, unfashionable and growing fast','Twelve people, no category, and a waiting list of enterprise buyers.','inherit','closed','closed','','210-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:39','2026-09-08 04:58:39','',210,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1448',0,'revision','',0),(1449,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Julien Marchetti what Bastion Freight does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Julien Marchetti’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight operates in freight forwarding from Marseille with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1328,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2edc6a19f8-1200x641.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1328\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Town_Skyline_captured_from_Bengal_Intelligent_Park,_Saltlake,_Kolkata_(2_of_2_photos).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Subhrajyoti07 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Bastion Freight lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Julien Marchetti spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The seed-stage company enterprise buyers keep calling','Procurement departments are not supposed to find you this early.','inherit','closed','closed','','212-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','',212,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1449',0,'revision','',0),(1450,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng does not do many interviews. The Accra office has no press page, Kindred Payments has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Amara Boateng laid out how Kindred Payments came to occupy the position it holds in payments: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1329,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8cf0d28ab3-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1329\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DLF_Epitome_Tower,_DLF_Phase_3,_Gurugram.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Sahil Dhiman · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in payments were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Kindred Payments did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Kindred Payments is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Kindred Payments navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Kindred Payments raised late, hired slowly and won anyway','A counter-cyclical playbook, executed with unusual patience.','inherit','closed','closed','','214-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','',214,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1450',0,'revision','',0),(1451,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Rohan Vaidya where the story stops being about Sextant Labs and starts being about developer tooling itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs was built in Pune on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Rohan Vaidya spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1330,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8ce8266747-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1330\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gurgaon_CyberHub.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Pithwilds · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in developer tooling depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Rohan Vaidya anticipated takes another five years, Sextant Labs has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Rohan Vaidya is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in developer tooling have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Rohan Vaidya’s timeline is the one variable Sextant Labs cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside the developer tooling startup rewriting a thirty-year-old workflow','The incumbents are not wrong about the problem. They are wrong about the shape of the answer.','inherit','closed','closed','','216-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','',216,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1451',0,'revision','',0),(1452,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim does not do many interviews. The Bergen office has no press page, Fjordline Materials has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Ingrid Solheim laid out how Fjordline Materials came to occupy the position it holds in advanced materials: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1331,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-a66b67ef58-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1331\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DLF_Cyber_City_with_Rapid_Metro,_Gurgaon.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in advanced materials were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Fjordline Materials did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Fjordline Materials is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Fjordline Materials navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Spotlight: Ingrid Solheim, Fjordline Materials','Our editors on why this founder is worth watching in advanced materials.','inherit','closed','closed','','218-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','',218,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1452',0,'revision','',0),(1453,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Caleb Whitfield where the story stops being about Ardent Mutual and starts being about insurance itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual was built in Toronto on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Caleb Whitfield spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1332,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-dfb8103782-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1332\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ripon_Building_panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">This Image was created by User:PlaneMad.\nIf you are using the image under the cr · CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in insurance depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Caleb Whitfield anticipated takes another five years, Ardent Mutual has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Caleb Whitfield is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in insurance have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Caleb Whitfield’s timeline is the one variable Ardent Mutual cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Five questions with Caleb Whitfield','On capital, hiring, competition and the decision that still keeps them up.','inherit','closed','closed','','220-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','',220,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1453',0,'revision','',0),(1454,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Lin Wei-Chen what Orbit Assembly does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Lin Wei-Chen’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly operates in industrial robotics from Taipei with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1333,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-058579f56b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1333\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chennai_train_station.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arian Zwegers\nderivative work: MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Orbit Assembly lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Lin Wei-Chen spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Lin Wei-Chen on the decision that defined Orbit Assembly','One call, made early, that everything since has been downstream of.','inherit','closed','closed','','222-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:40','2026-09-08 04:58:40','',222,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1454',0,'revision','',0),(1455,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois does not do many interviews. The Montreal office has no press page, Cellar Six has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Nadia Bourgeois laid out how Cellar Six came to occupy the position it holds in food and beverage: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1334,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-c7a2f18759-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1334\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_SE_Island_Grounds_Chennai_Jul25_R16_07915.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Timothy A. Gonsalves · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in food and beverage were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Cellar Six did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Cellar Six is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Cellar Six navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The entrepreneur our editors are watching: Nadia Bourgeois','Building steadily in Montreal, in a category the market has not priced yet.','inherit','closed','closed','','224-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','',224,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1455',0,'revision','',0),(1456,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Tomas Novak what Vesper Systems does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Tomas Novak’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems operates in building automation from Prague with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1335,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-bf7da0674c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1335\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Vesper Systems lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Tomas Novak spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Vesper Systems, profiled: model, margins and momentum','What the business actually is, underneath the positioning.','inherit','closed','closed','','226-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','',226,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1456',0,'revision','',0),(1457,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour does not do many interviews. The Amman office has no press page, Rafiq Capital has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Aisha Mansour laid out how Rafiq Capital came to occupy the position it holds in small-business lending: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive, Rafiq Capital</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1336,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-d6af6b95ea-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1336\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Awfis_-_Collab_Zone.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Anniefernandes · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in small-business lending were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Rafiq Capital did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Rafiq Capital is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Rafiq Capital navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','What Rafiq Capital sells — and to whom','A clear-eyed look at the customer base behind the growth rate.','inherit','closed','closed','','228-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','',228,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1457',0,'revision','',0),(1458,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Gregor Lindqvist where the story stops being about Baltic Yield and starts being about agricultural technology itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield was built in Malmö on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Gregor Lindqvist spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1337,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-83860a1e13-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1337\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BHIVE_Workspace_-_HSR_Bangalore.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Coworker2016 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in agricultural technology depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Gregor Lindqvist anticipated takes another five years, Baltic Yield has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Gregor Lindqvist is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in agricultural technology have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Gregor Lindqvist’s timeline is the one variable Baltic Yield cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Baltic Yield at a glance: the numbers behind the narrative','Revenue mix, retention, headcount and the trajectory they imply.','inherit','closed','closed','','230-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','',230,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1458',0,'revision','',0),(1459,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Chiara Bellandi what Atelier Nove does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Chiara Bellandi’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove operates in design manufacturing from Milan with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1338,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-31e0fe1f5d-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1338\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guest_seating_and_planter_boxes_inside_an_office,_Filmnagar_(1).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Atelier Nove lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Chiara Bellandi spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Profile: how Atelier Nove makes money in design manufacturing','Three revenue lines, one of which is quietly becoming the business.','inherit','closed','closed','','232-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','',232,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1459',0,'revision','',0),(1460,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most early-stage advice is written for companies with a large addressable market. Samuel Adeyemi built Longbridge Learning for a market of roughly forty buyers, which makes almost all of it useless — and produced a rather different playbook.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Nairobi company sells into a narrow segment of workforce training where the entire universe of potential customers is known, named and reachable. There is no funnel. There is a list, and a set of relationships at various stages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you can name every customer you will ever have, growth stops being a marketing problem and becomes a sequencing problem.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1355,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-people-6e7ef56dee-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"People at work in an Indian business setting\" class=\"wp-image-1355\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Early-stage companies run on far less than the narrative suggests. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Part_of_the_audience_-_Wikiconference_India_2023_-_Hyderabad_2023-04-28.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Rangan Datta Wiki · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Selling into a known universe</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are unrecognisable to most early-stage teams. There is no demand generation, because demand is fixed. Positioning matters enormously, because every buyer will eventually talk to every other buyer. And the cost of a bad implementation is not churn — it is the permanent loss of a meaningful share of the total market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Longbridge Learning therefore sequenced its first customers deliberately, starting with two organisations that were technically demanding, politically influential and unlikely to be easy. Both took longer than planned. Both now function as references that shorten every subsequent conversation by months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, customer sequencing is the growth strategy.</li>\n<li>A poor early implementation removes a percentage of the total market permanently.</li>\n<li>Reference customers compound faster when buyers already know each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Samuel Adeyemi concedes the model has a ceiling, and has begun mapping adjacent segments with similar characteristics. For now, though, Longbridge Learning is a useful reminder that a great deal of standard early-stage practice assumes a market shape that not every company has.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The first ten customers of Longbridge Learning','Who they were, what they paid, and what they demanded in return.','inherit','closed','closed','','234-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','',234,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1460',0,'revision','',0),(1461,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The funding announcement for Steinweg Logistik was deliberately modest: a Series A, sized to eighteen months rather than thirty-six, from investors Hannah Vogel had been talking to for two years before there was anything to invest in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Hamburg company operates in warehousing, a category that attracted a great deal of capital three years ago and rather less since. Hannah Vogel watched several better-funded competitors raise at valuations that have since become a problem for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>A big round solves this quarter and creates every quarter after it. We wanted a round that solved a specific thing.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1356,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-people-92fde4c4aa-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"People at work in an Indian business setting\" class=\"wp-image-1356\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The first ten customers decide the shape of everything after. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satnam_Singh_Sandhu_-_Katherine_Maher_-_Surjit_Patar_-_Press_Conference_-_Wiki_Conference_India_-_CGC_-_Mohali_2016-08-05_6725.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the money is for</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The use of funds is unusually specific: two engineers, a delivery lead, and a twelve-month runway extension. There is no plan to build a sales organisation, on the grounds that Steinweg Logistik has not yet worked out what a repeatable sale looks like and would only be hiring people to discover that expensively.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That reasoning reflects a broader shift in the early-stage market. Rounds are smaller, processes are longer, and investors are asking to see retention data that simply did not get requested three years ago. Founders describe the change as uncomfortable and, in most cases, healthy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The state of the market</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Round size is a strategic decision that constrains the next two years, not just this one.</li>\n<li>Hiring a sales team before the sale is repeatable buys expensive research.</li>\n<li>Retention data is now standard diligence at seed and Series A, not just later stages.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hannah Vogel is realistic about what the round changes. It buys time and two hires. Whether Steinweg Logistik converts that into a repeatable motion is a question the next four quarters will answer, and one no amount of capital would have answered faster.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Steinweg Logistik launched to silence. Month seven changed that.','The story of a launch that failed and a business that did not.','inherit','closed','closed','','236-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:41','2026-09-08 04:58:41','',236,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1461',0,'revision','',0),(1462,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ribera Foods has twelve employees, no marketing function and a product that fits on a single page. It also has a waiting list of food distribution buyers, which is not how any of this is supposed to work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo started the company in Bogotá after several years working inside the problem. The first version was built for a former employer, sold for a modest fee, and only became a company when three other organisations asked for the same thing within a month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We never had to find product-market fit. We had to work out whether the fit was big enough to be a business.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1357,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-people-aa19be3a5b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"People at work in an Indian business setting\" class=\"wp-image-1357\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Product decisions, made with incomplete information. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satnam_Singh_Sandhu_-_Katherine_Maher_-_Surjit_Patar_-_Press_Conference_-_Wiki_Conference_India_-_CGC_-_Mohali_2016-08-05_6801.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why buyers arrived early</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enterprise procurement is not supposed to engage with twelve-person companies. It does so here because the alternative is a two-year internal project with an uncertain outcome, and because Ribera Foods solves a problem specific enough that the build-versus-buy calculation is genuinely lopsided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The constraint is delivery. Each implementation currently requires Diego Salcedo or one of two other people, which caps the company at a handful of new customers per quarter. The obvious answer is to hire; the reason it has not happened is that the implementation process is still changing shape every few months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The open questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow problems make build-versus-buy decisions easy, which is why small companies win them.</li>\n<li>Implementation capacity, not demand, is the binding constraint on most early enterprise businesses.</li>\n<li>Hiring into a process that is still changing usually produces two problems instead of one.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo expects to raise within the year, largely to fund a delivery team. The more interesting question is whether Ribera Foods can codify what it does well enough that someone other than its founders can do it — the transition that decides most companies at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Diego Salcedo found fit in a market of forty buyers','When the total addressable market is a spreadsheet, the playbook changes.','inherit','closed','closed','','238-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','',238,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1462',0,'revision','',0),(1463,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most early-stage advice is written for companies with a large addressable market. Meera Krishnan built Aster Fieldworks for a market of roughly forty buyers, which makes almost all of it useless — and produced a rather different playbook.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Chennai company sells into a narrow segment of field service software where the entire universe of potential customers is known, named and reachable. There is no funnel. There is a list, and a set of relationships at various stages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you can name every customer you will ever have, growth stops being a marketing problem and becomes a sequencing problem.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1358,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-people-210b19b425-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"People at work in an Indian business setting\" class=\"wp-image-1358\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Early-stage companies run on far less than the narrative suggests. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satnam_Singh_Sandhu_and_Katherine_Maher_-_Press_Conference_-_Wiki_Conference_India_-_CGC_-_Mohali_2016-08-05_6723.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Selling into a known universe</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are unrecognisable to most early-stage teams. There is no demand generation, because demand is fixed. Positioning matters enormously, because every buyer will eventually talk to every other buyer. And the cost of a bad implementation is not churn — it is the permanent loss of a meaningful share of the total market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aster Fieldworks therefore sequenced its first customers deliberately, starting with two organisations that were technically demanding, politically influential and unlikely to be easy. Both took longer than planned. Both now function as references that shorten every subsequent conversation by months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, customer sequencing is the growth strategy.</li>\n<li>A poor early implementation removes a percentage of the total market permanently.</li>\n<li>Reference customers compound faster when buyers already know each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meera Krishnan concedes the model has a ceiling, and has begun mapping adjacent segments with similar characteristics. For now, though, Aster Fieldworks is a useful reminder that a great deal of standard early-stage practice assumes a market shape that not every company has.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The startup that grew by shrinking its product','Cutting features turned out to be the fastest route to a bigger contract.','inherit','closed','closed','','240-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','',240,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1463',0,'revision','',0),(1464,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Trellis Property has twelve employees, no marketing function and a product that fits on a single page. It also has a waiting list of commercial real estate buyers, which is not how any of this is supposed to work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth started the company in Sydney after several years working inside the problem. The first version was built for a former employer, sold for a modest fee, and only became a company when three other organisations asked for the same thing within a month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We never had to find product-market fit. We had to work out whether the fit was big enough to be a business.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1359,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-people-345df809b1-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"People at work in an Indian business setting\" class=\"wp-image-1359\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The first ten customers decide the shape of everything after. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satnam_Singh_Sandhu_and_Katherine_Maher_-_Press_Conference_-_Wiki_Conference_India_-_CGC_-_Mohali_2016-08-05_6724.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why buyers arrived early</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enterprise procurement is not supposed to engage with twelve-person companies. It does so here because the alternative is a two-year internal project with an uncertain outcome, and because Trellis Property solves a problem specific enough that the build-versus-buy calculation is genuinely lopsided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The constraint is delivery. Each implementation currently requires Oliver Ashworth or one of two other people, which caps the company at a handful of new customers per quarter. The obvious answer is to hire; the reason it has not happened is that the implementation process is still changing shape every few months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The open questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow problems make build-versus-buy decisions easy, which is why small companies win them.</li>\n<li>Implementation capacity, not demand, is the binding constraint on most early enterprise businesses.</li>\n<li>Hiring into a process that is still changing usually produces two problems instead of one.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth expects to raise within the year, largely to fund a delivery team. The more interesting question is whether Trellis Property can codify what it does well enough that someone other than its founders can do it — the transition that decides most companies at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Trellis Property raises a Series A to expand its commercial real estate platform','The round is modest by recent standards, and deliberately so.','inherit','closed','closed','','242-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','',242,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1464',0,'revision','',0),(1465,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most early-stage advice is written for companies with a large addressable market. Fatima Al-Rashid built Nawa Health for a market of roughly forty buyers, which makes almost all of it useless — and produced a rather different playbook.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Dubai company sells into a narrow segment of digital health where the entire universe of potential customers is known, named and reachable. There is no funnel. There is a list, and a set of relationships at various stages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you can name every customer you will ever have, growth stops being a marketing problem and becomes a sequencing problem.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1360,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-people-ceb8d146e3-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"People at work in an Indian business setting\" class=\"wp-image-1360\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Product decisions, made with incomplete information. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Press_Conference_-_Wiki_Conference_India_-_CGC_-_Mohali_2016-08-05_6798.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Selling into a known universe</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are unrecognisable to most early-stage teams. There is no demand generation, because demand is fixed. Positioning matters enormously, because every buyer will eventually talk to every other buyer. And the cost of a bad implementation is not churn — it is the permanent loss of a meaningful share of the total market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health therefore sequenced its first customers deliberately, starting with two organisations that were technically demanding, politically influential and unlikely to be easy. Both took longer than planned. Both now function as references that shorten every subsequent conversation by months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, customer sequencing is the growth strategy.</li>\n<li>A poor early implementation removes a percentage of the total market permanently.</li>\n<li>Reference customers compound faster when buyers already know each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fatima Al-Rashid concedes the model has a ceiling, and has begun mapping adjacent segments with similar characteristics. For now, though, Nawa Health is a useful reminder that a great deal of standard early-stage practice assumes a market shape that not every company has.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Seed rounds are smaller and slower. Founders are adapting.','Longer processes, tighter terms, and a visible shift in what gets funded.','inherit','closed','closed','','244-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','',244,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1465',0,'revision','',0),(1466,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The funding announcement for Kestrel Defence Systems was deliberately modest: a Series A, sized to eighteen months rather than thirty-six, from investors Viktor Marchenko had been talking to for two years before there was anything to invest in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Warsaw company operates in aerospace components, a category that attracted a great deal of capital three years ago and rather less since. Viktor Marchenko watched several better-funded competitors raise at valuations that have since become a problem for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>A big round solves this quarter and creates every quarter after it. We wanted a round that solved a specific thing.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1384,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-792679a914-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1384\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Early-stage companies run on far less than the narrative suggests. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ETV-Digital-Studio-Arlaka-Sanjay-Priyanke.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Priyanke · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the money is for</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The use of funds is unusually specific: two engineers, a delivery lead, and a twelve-month runway extension. There is no plan to build a sales organisation, on the grounds that Kestrel Defence Systems has not yet worked out what a repeatable sale looks like and would only be hiring people to discover that expensively.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That reasoning reflects a broader shift in the early-stage market. Rounds are smaller, processes are longer, and investors are asking to see retention data that simply did not get requested three years ago. Founders describe the change as uncomfortable and, in most cases, healthy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The state of the market</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Round size is a strategic decision that constrains the next two years, not just this one.</li>\n<li>Hiring a sales team before the sale is repeatable buys expensive research.</li>\n<li>Retention data is now standard diligence at seed and Series A, not just later stages.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko is realistic about what the round changes. It buys time and two hires. Whether Kestrel Defence Systems converts that into a repeatable motion is a question the next four quarters will answer, and one no amount of capital would have answered faster.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Kestrel Defence Systems acquires a competitor to buy time, not revenue','The target’s customers were incidental. Its engineering team was not.','inherit','closed','closed','','246-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','',246,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1466',0,'revision','',0),(1467,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Harbourline Retail has twelve employees, no marketing function and a product that fits on a single page. It also has a waiting list of omnichannel retail buyers, which is not how any of this is supposed to work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura started the company in Osaka after several years working inside the problem. The first version was built for a former employer, sold for a modest fee, and only became a company when three other organisations asked for the same thing within a month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We never had to find product-market fit. We had to work out whether the fit was big enough to be a business.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1385,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-a8a096bcf2-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1385\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The first ten customers decide the shape of everything after. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MDR_Kripo_live.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why buyers arrived early</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enterprise procurement is not supposed to engage with twelve-person companies. It does so here because the alternative is a two-year internal project with an uncertain outcome, and because Harbourline Retail solves a problem specific enough that the build-versus-buy calculation is genuinely lopsided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The constraint is delivery. Each implementation currently requires Grace Nakamura or one of two other people, which caps the company at a handful of new customers per quarter. The obvious answer is to hire; the reason it has not happened is that the implementation process is still changing shape every few months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The open questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow problems make build-versus-buy decisions easy, which is why small companies win them.</li>\n<li>Implementation capacity, not demand, is the binding constraint on most early enterprise businesses.</li>\n<li>Hiring into a process that is still changing usually produces two problems instead of one.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura expects to raise within the year, largely to fund a delivery team. The more interesting question is whether Harbourline Retail can codify what it does well enough that someone other than its founders can do it — the transition that decides most companies at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Early-stage funding held steady last quarter — with a catch','The totals look flat. The distribution underneath them does not.','inherit','closed','closed','','248-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:42','2026-09-08 04:58:42','',248,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1467',0,'revision','',0),(1468,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The funding announcement for Meridian Loop was deliberately modest: a Series A, sized to eighteen months rather than thirty-six, from investors Priya Raghunathan had been talking to for two years before there was anything to invest in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Singapore company operates in logistics software, a category that attracted a great deal of capital three years ago and rather less since. Priya Raghunathan watched several better-funded competitors raise at valuations that have since become a problem for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>A big round solves this quarter and creates every quarter after it. We wanted a round that solved a specific thing.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1386,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-fed33a1a5b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1386\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Product decisions, made with incomplete information. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5015.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the money is for</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The use of funds is unusually specific: two engineers, a delivery lead, and a twelve-month runway extension. There is no plan to build a sales organisation, on the grounds that Meridian Loop has not yet worked out what a repeatable sale looks like and would only be hiring people to discover that expensively.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That reasoning reflects a broader shift in the early-stage market. Rounds are smaller, processes are longer, and investors are asking to see retention data that simply did not get requested three years ago. Founders describe the change as uncomfortable and, in most cases, healthy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The state of the market</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Round size is a strategic decision that constrains the next two years, not just this one.</li>\n<li>Hiring a sales team before the sale is repeatable buys expensive research.</li>\n<li>Retention data is now standard diligence at seed and Series A, not just later stages.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan is realistic about what the round changes. It buys time and two hires. Whether Meridian Loop converts that into a repeatable motion is a question the next four quarters will answer, and one no amount of capital would have answered faster.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Priya Raghunathan left a comfortable job to build Meridian Loop','The maths of quitting, and the eighteen months that followed.','inherit','closed','closed','','250-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','',250,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1468',0,'revision','',0),(1469,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Halden Grid has twelve employees, no marketing function and a product that fits on a single page. It also has a waiting list of energy infrastructure buyers, which is not how any of this is supposed to work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo started the company in Lagos after several years working inside the problem. The first version was built for a former employer, sold for a modest fee, and only became a company when three other organisations asked for the same thing within a month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We never had to find product-market fit. We had to work out whether the fit was big enough to be a business.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1387,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-33e233ca8c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1387\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Early-stage companies run on far less than the narrative suggests. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5017.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why buyers arrived early</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enterprise procurement is not supposed to engage with twelve-person companies. It does so here because the alternative is a two-year internal project with an uncertain outcome, and because Halden Grid solves a problem specific enough that the build-versus-buy calculation is genuinely lopsided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The constraint is delivery. Each implementation currently requires Daniel Okonkwo or one of two other people, which caps the company at a handful of new customers per quarter. The obvious answer is to hire; the reason it has not happened is that the implementation process is still changing shape every few months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The open questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow problems make build-versus-buy decisions easy, which is why small companies win them.</li>\n<li>Implementation capacity, not demand, is the binding constraint on most early enterprise businesses.</li>\n<li>Hiring into a process that is still changing usually produces two problems instead of one.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo expects to raise within the year, largely to fund a delivery team. The more interesting question is whether Halden Grid can codify what it does well enough that someone other than its founders can do it — the transition that decides most companies at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The founder skill nobody teaches: sequencing','Knowing what is true is easy. Knowing what to do first is the job.','inherit','closed','closed','','252-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','',252,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1469',0,'revision','',0),(1470,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most early-stage advice is written for companies with a large addressable market. Marta Ferreira built Quorum Health for a market of roughly forty buyers, which makes almost all of it useless — and produced a rather different playbook.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Lisbon company sells into a narrow segment of clinical operations where the entire universe of potential customers is known, named and reachable. There is no funnel. There is a list, and a set of relationships at various stages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you can name every customer you will ever have, growth stops being a marketing problem and becomes a sequencing problem.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1388,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-38c24301b8-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1388\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The first ten customers decide the shape of everything after. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5019.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Selling into a known universe</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are unrecognisable to most early-stage teams. There is no demand generation, because demand is fixed. Positioning matters enormously, because every buyer will eventually talk to every other buyer. And the cost of a bad implementation is not churn — it is the permanent loss of a meaningful share of the total market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health therefore sequenced its first customers deliberately, starting with two organisations that were technically demanding, politically influential and unlikely to be easy. Both took longer than planned. Both now function as references that shorten every subsequent conversation by months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, customer sequencing is the growth strategy.</li>\n<li>A poor early implementation removes a percentage of the total market permanently.</li>\n<li>Reference customers compound faster when buyers already know each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Marta Ferreira concedes the model has a ceiling, and has begun mapping adjacent segments with similar characteristics. For now, though, Quorum Health is a useful reminder that a great deal of standard early-stage practice assumes a market shape that not every company has.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Marta Ferreira on fundraising without a network','Cold outreach, a bad first process, and a much better second one.','inherit','closed','closed','','254-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','',254,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1470',0,'revision','',0),(1471,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The funding announcement for Northgate Analytics was deliberately modest: a Series A, sized to eighteen months rather than thirty-six, from investors Ethan Brightwell had been talking to for two years before there was anything to invest in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Manchester company operates in risk analytics, a category that attracted a great deal of capital three years ago and rather less since. Ethan Brightwell watched several better-funded competitors raise at valuations that have since become a problem for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>A big round solves this quarter and creates every quarter after it. We wanted a round that solved a specific thing.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1389,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-f3acfaeb97-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1389\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Product decisions, made with incomplete information. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5020.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the money is for</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The use of funds is unusually specific: two engineers, a delivery lead, and a twelve-month runway extension. There is no plan to build a sales organisation, on the grounds that Northgate Analytics has not yet worked out what a repeatable sale looks like and would only be hiring people to discover that expensively.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That reasoning reflects a broader shift in the early-stage market. Rounds are smaller, processes are longer, and investors are asking to see retention data that simply did not get requested three years ago. Founders describe the change as uncomfortable and, in most cases, healthy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The state of the market</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Round size is a strategic decision that constrains the next two years, not just this one.</li>\n<li>Hiring a sales team before the sale is repeatable buys expensive research.</li>\n<li>Retention data is now standard diligence at seed and Series A, not just later stages.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell is realistic about what the round changes. It buys time and two hires. Whether Northgate Analytics converts that into a repeatable motion is a question the next four quarters will answer, and one no amount of capital would have answered faster.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Two founders, one product, and a rule about disagreement','How Northgate Analytics’s founding team decided who decides.','inherit','closed','closed','','256-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','',256,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1471',0,'revision','',0),(1472,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most early-stage advice is written for companies with a large addressable market. Sana Qureshi built Tallow & Co. for a market of roughly forty buyers, which makes almost all of it useless — and produced a rather different playbook.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Karachi company sells into a narrow segment of consumer manufacturing where the entire universe of potential customers is known, named and reachable. There is no funnel. There is a list, and a set of relationships at various stages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you can name every customer you will ever have, growth stops being a marketing problem and becomes a sequencing problem.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1390,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-4fc53455ed-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1390\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Early-stage companies run on far less than the narrative suggests. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5021.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Selling into a known universe</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are unrecognisable to most early-stage teams. There is no demand generation, because demand is fixed. Positioning matters enormously, because every buyer will eventually talk to every other buyer. And the cost of a bad implementation is not churn — it is the permanent loss of a meaningful share of the total market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. therefore sequenced its first customers deliberately, starting with two organisations that were technically demanding, politically influential and unlikely to be easy. Both took longer than planned. Both now function as references that shorten every subsequent conversation by months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, customer sequencing is the growth strategy.</li>\n<li>A poor early implementation removes a percentage of the total market permanently.</li>\n<li>Reference customers compound faster when buyers already know each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sana Qureshi concedes the model has a ceiling, and has begun mapping adjacent segments with similar characteristics. For now, though, Tallow & Co. is a useful reminder that a great deal of standard early-stage practice assumes a market shape that not every company has.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Tallow & Co. went from seed to profitable in thirty-one months','Not by growing faster. By spending like the money might not come again.','inherit','closed','closed','','258-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','',258,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1472',0,'revision','',0),(1473,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The funding announcement for Bastion Freight was deliberately modest: a Series A, sized to eighteen months rather than thirty-six, from investors Julien Marchetti had been talking to for two years before there was anything to invest in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Marseille company operates in freight forwarding, a category that attracted a great deal of capital three years ago and rather less since. Julien Marchetti watched several better-funded competitors raise at valuations that have since become a problem for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>A big round solves this quarter and creates every quarter after it. We wanted a round that solved a specific thing.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1391,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-f994194768-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1391\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The first ten customers decide the shape of everything after. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5022.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the money is for</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The use of funds is unusually specific: two engineers, a delivery lead, and a twelve-month runway extension. There is no plan to build a sales organisation, on the grounds that Bastion Freight has not yet worked out what a repeatable sale looks like and would only be hiring people to discover that expensively.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That reasoning reflects a broader shift in the early-stage market. Rounds are smaller, processes are longer, and investors are asking to see retention data that simply did not get requested three years ago. Founders describe the change as uncomfortable and, in most cases, healthy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The state of the market</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Round size is a strategic decision that constrains the next two years, not just this one.</li>\n<li>Hiring a sales team before the sale is repeatable buys expensive research.</li>\n<li>Retention data is now standard diligence at seed and Series A, not just later stages.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti is realistic about what the round changes. It buys time and two hires. Whether Bastion Freight converts that into a repeatable motion is a question the next four quarters will answer, and one no amount of capital would have answered faster.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The startup that turned a support burden into its moat','The most expensive part of the business became the part nobody can copy.','inherit','closed','closed','','260-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','',260,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1473',0,'revision','',0),(1474,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Kindred Payments has twelve employees, no marketing function and a product that fits on a single page. It also has a waiting list of payments buyers, which is not how any of this is supposed to work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng started the company in Accra after several years working inside the problem. The first version was built for a former employer, sold for a modest fee, and only became a company when three other organisations asked for the same thing within a month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We never had to find product-market fit. We had to work out whether the fit was big enough to be a business.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1392,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-1a73a461cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1392\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Product decisions, made with incomplete information. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5041.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why buyers arrived early</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enterprise procurement is not supposed to engage with twelve-person companies. It does so here because the alternative is a two-year internal project with an uncertain outcome, and because Kindred Payments solves a problem specific enough that the build-versus-buy calculation is genuinely lopsided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The constraint is delivery. Each implementation currently requires Amara Boateng or one of two other people, which caps the company at a handful of new customers per quarter. The obvious answer is to hire; the reason it has not happened is that the implementation process is still changing shape every few months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The open questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow problems make build-versus-buy decisions easy, which is why small companies win them.</li>\n<li>Implementation capacity, not demand, is the binding constraint on most early enterprise businesses.</li>\n<li>Hiring into a process that is still changing usually produces two problems instead of one.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng expects to raise within the year, largely to fund a delivery team. The more interesting question is whether Kindred Payments can codify what it does well enough that someone other than its founders can do it — the transition that decides most companies at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Kindred Payments’s first million: slower and stranger than planned','Three customers, one of them accidental, and a lesson about concentration.','inherit','closed','closed','','262-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:43','2026-09-08 04:58:43','',262,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1474',0,'revision','',0),(1475,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most early-stage advice is written for companies with a large addressable market. Rohan Vaidya built Sextant Labs for a market of roughly forty buyers, which makes almost all of it useless — and produced a rather different playbook.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Pune company sells into a narrow segment of developer tooling where the entire universe of potential customers is known, named and reachable. There is no funnel. There is a list, and a set of relationships at various stages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you can name every customer you will ever have, growth stops being a marketing problem and becomes a sequencing problem.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1393,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-7a38ed2796-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1393\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Early-stage companies run on far less than the narrative suggests. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5042.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Selling into a known universe</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are unrecognisable to most early-stage teams. There is no demand generation, because demand is fixed. Positioning matters enormously, because every buyer will eventually talk to every other buyer. And the cost of a bad implementation is not churn — it is the permanent loss of a meaningful share of the total market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs therefore sequenced its first customers deliberately, starting with two organisations that were technically demanding, politically influential and unlikely to be easy. Both took longer than planned. Both now function as references that shorten every subsequent conversation by months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, customer sequencing is the growth strategy.</li>\n<li>A poor early implementation removes a percentage of the total market permanently.</li>\n<li>Reference customers compound faster when buyers already know each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rohan Vaidya concedes the model has a ceiling, and has begun mapping adjacent segments with similar characteristics. For now, though, Sextant Labs is a useful reminder that a great deal of standard early-stage practice assumes a market shape that not every company has.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Sextant Labs beat a better-funded rival','Less capital, fewer people, and a much clearer idea of who the customer was.','inherit','closed','closed','','264-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','',264,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1475',0,'revision','',0),(1476,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fjordline Materials has twelve employees, no marketing function and a product that fits on a single page. It also has a waiting list of advanced materials buyers, which is not how any of this is supposed to work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim started the company in Bergen after several years working inside the problem. The first version was built for a former employer, sold for a modest fee, and only became a company when three other organisations asked for the same thing within a month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We never had to find product-market fit. We had to work out whether the fit was big enough to be a business.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1394,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-8fc8b6c927-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1394\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The first ten customers decide the shape of everything after. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amazon_Pavilion_-_41st_International_Kolkata_Book_Fair_-_Milan_Mela_Complex_-_Kolkata_2017-02-04_5101.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why buyers arrived early</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enterprise procurement is not supposed to engage with twelve-person companies. It does so here because the alternative is a two-year internal project with an uncertain outcome, and because Fjordline Materials solves a problem specific enough that the build-versus-buy calculation is genuinely lopsided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The constraint is delivery. Each implementation currently requires Ingrid Solheim or one of two other people, which caps the company at a handful of new customers per quarter. The obvious answer is to hire; the reason it has not happened is that the implementation process is still changing shape every few months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The open questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow problems make build-versus-buy decisions easy, which is why small companies win them.</li>\n<li>Implementation capacity, not demand, is the binding constraint on most early enterprise businesses.</li>\n<li>Hiring into a process that is still changing usually produces two problems instead of one.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim expects to raise within the year, largely to fund a delivery team. The more interesting question is whether Fjordline Materials can codify what it does well enough that someone other than its founders can do it — the transition that decides most companies at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The benchmarks founders actually need in year one','Most early-stage metrics are noise. These four are not.','inherit','closed','closed','','266-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','',266,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1476',0,'revision','',0),(1477,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most early-stage advice is written for companies with a large addressable market. Caleb Whitfield built Ardent Mutual for a market of roughly forty buyers, which makes almost all of it useless — and produced a rather different playbook.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Toronto company sells into a narrow segment of insurance where the entire universe of potential customers is known, named and reachable. There is no funnel. There is a list, and a set of relationships at various stages.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>When you can name every customer you will ever have, growth stops being a marketing problem and becomes a sequencing problem.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1395,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-media-7a751901ed-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Media, press and publishing in India\" class=\"wp-image-1395\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Product decisions, made with incomplete information. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_Bank_of_India_Chairman%27s_Press_Conference_-_Kolkata_2011-05-17_0318.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Selling into a known universe</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics are unrecognisable to most early-stage teams. There is no demand generation, because demand is fixed. Positioning matters enormously, because every buyer will eventually talk to every other buyer. And the cost of a bad implementation is not churn — it is the permanent loss of a meaningful share of the total market.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual therefore sequenced its first customers deliberately, starting with two organisations that were technically demanding, politically influential and unlikely to be easy. Both took longer than planned. Both now function as references that shorten every subsequent conversation by months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, customer sequencing is the growth strategy.</li>\n<li>A poor early implementation removes a percentage of the total market permanently.</li>\n<li>Reference customers compound faster when buyers already know each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Caleb Whitfield concedes the model has a ceiling, and has begun mapping adjacent segments with similar characteristics. For now, though, Ardent Mutual is a useful reminder that a great deal of standard early-stage practice assumes a market shape that not every company has.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The numbers that mislead early-stage teams','Vanity metrics do not just flatter — they redirect the roadmap.','inherit','closed','closed','','268-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','',268,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1477',0,'revision','',0),(1478,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The funding announcement for Orbit Assembly was deliberately modest: a Series A, sized to eighteen months rather than thirty-six, from investors Lin Wei-Chen had been talking to for two years before there was anything to invest in.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Taipei company operates in industrial robotics, a category that attracted a great deal of capital three years ago and rather less since. Lin Wei-Chen watched several better-funded competitors raise at valuations that have since become a problem for them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>A big round solves this quarter and creates every quarter after it. We wanted a round that solved a specific thing.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1336,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-d6af6b95ea-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1336\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Early-stage companies run on far less than the narrative suggests. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Awfis_-_Collab_Zone.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Anniefernandes · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the money is for</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The use of funds is unusually specific: two engineers, a delivery lead, and a twelve-month runway extension. There is no plan to build a sales organisation, on the grounds that Orbit Assembly has not yet worked out what a repeatable sale looks like and would only be hiring people to discover that expensively.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That reasoning reflects a broader shift in the early-stage market. Rounds are smaller, processes are longer, and investors are asking to see retention data that simply did not get requested three years ago. Founders describe the change as uncomfortable and, in most cases, healthy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The state of the market</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Round size is a strategic decision that constrains the next two years, not just this one.</li>\n<li>Hiring a sales team before the sale is repeatable buys expensive research.</li>\n<li>Retention data is now standard diligence at seed and Series A, not just later stages.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen is realistic about what the round changes. It buys time and two hires. Whether Orbit Assembly converts that into a repeatable motion is a question the next four quarters will answer, and one no amount of capital would have answered faster.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Hiring your first ten: what Lin Wei-Chen would do differently','Slower, more expensive, and far more specific about the first three.','inherit','closed','closed','','270-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','',270,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1478',0,'revision','',0),(1479,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Cellar Six has twelve employees, no marketing function and a product that fits on a single page. It also has a waiting list of food and beverage buyers, which is not how any of this is supposed to work.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois started the company in Montreal after several years working inside the problem. The first version was built for a former employer, sold for a modest fee, and only became a company when three other organisations asked for the same thing within a month.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We never had to find product-market fit. We had to work out whether the fit was big enough to be a business.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1337,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-83860a1e13-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1337\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The first ten customers decide the shape of everything after. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BHIVE_Workspace_-_HSR_Bangalore.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Coworker2016 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why buyers arrived early</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Enterprise procurement is not supposed to engage with twelve-person companies. It does so here because the alternative is a two-year internal project with an uncertain outcome, and because Cellar Six solves a problem specific enough that the build-versus-buy calculation is genuinely lopsided.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The constraint is delivery. Each implementation currently requires Nadia Bourgeois or one of two other people, which caps the company at a handful of new customers per quarter. The obvious answer is to hire; the reason it has not happened is that the implementation process is still changing shape every few months.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The open questions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow problems make build-versus-buy decisions easy, which is why small companies win them.</li>\n<li>Implementation capacity, not demand, is the binding constraint on most early enterprise businesses.</li>\n<li>Hiring into a process that is still changing usually produces two problems instead of one.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois expects to raise within the year, largely to fund a delivery team. The more interesting question is whether Cellar Six can codify what it does well enough that someone other than its founders can do it — the transition that decides most companies at this stage.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Pricing is a product decision. Treat it like one.','The most under-resourced lever in early-stage companies, examined properly.','inherit','closed','closed','','272-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','',272,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1479',0,'revision','',0),(1480,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Vesper Systems took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Tomas Novak describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Prague business had a real problem: in building automation, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place_sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ville Miettinen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Vesper Systems was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Tomas Novak regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Search changed. Vesper Systems’s acquisition model had to change with it.','A rebuild of the demand engine, done while the numbers were still working.','inherit','closed','closed','','274-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','',274,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1480',0,'revision','',0),(1481,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Rafiq Capital could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Aisha Mansour has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Amman company operates in small-business lending, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive, Rafiq Capital</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(26528).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Rafiq Capital rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in small-business lending are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The channel mix that works when paid gets expensive','What companies do when the reliable channel stops being reliable.','inherit','closed','closed','','276-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:44','2026-09-08 04:58:44','',276,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1481',0,'revision','',0),(1482,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Gregor Lindqvist publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Baltic Yield’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Malmö company sells into agricultural technology, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(37550).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Baltic Yield stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Gregor Lindqvist is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for agricultural technology businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Gregor Lindqvist on measuring marketing you cannot attribute','Giving up on perfect attribution turned out to improve the decisions.','inherit','closed','closed','','278-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','',278,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1482',0,'revision','',0),(1483,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Atelier Nove took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Chiara Bellandi describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Milan business had a real problem: in design manufacturing, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(47183).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Atelier Nove was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Chiara Bellandi regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Content still works — just not the way it did','Volume stopped paying. Specificity started.','inherit','closed','closed','','280-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','',280,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1483',0,'revision','',0),(1484,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Samuel Adeyemi publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Longbridge Learning’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Nairobi company sells into workforce training, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(80844).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Longbridge Learning stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Samuel Adeyemi is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for workforce training businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Rebrands fail for boring reasons. Longbridge Learning avoided them.','Not a logo project: a positioning decision with an identity attached.','inherit','closed','closed','','282-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','',282,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1484',0,'revision','',0),(1485,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Steinweg Logistik took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Hannah Vogel describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Hamburg business had a real problem: in warehousing, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1322,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-d46d8e78c5-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1322\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City_buildings_(30087).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Steinweg Logistik was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Hannah Vogel regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Positioning is the cheapest growth lever most companies ignore','It costs a quarter of thinking and changes every downstream number.','inherit','closed','closed','','284-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','',284,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1485',0,'revision','',0),(1486,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Ribera Foods could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Diego Salcedo has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Bogotá company operates in food distribution, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(98608).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Ribera Foods rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in food distribution are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Ribera Foods built a brand in an unbranded category','When competitors sound identical, being legible is the entire advantage.','inherit','closed','closed','','286-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','',286,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1486',0,'revision','',0),(1487,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meera Krishnan publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Aster Fieldworks’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Chennai company sells into field service software, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1324,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ae876be62e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1324\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemon_Tree_hotel_in_HITEC_city.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Aster Fieldworks stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meera Krishnan is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for field service software businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Meera Krishnan: “We stopped describing what we do.”','Describing who it is for turned out to be far more useful.','inherit','closed','closed','','288-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:45','2026-09-08 04:58:45','',288,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1487',0,'revision','',0),(1488,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Trellis Property could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Oliver Ashworth has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Sydney company operates in commercial real estate, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1325,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-60852483f6-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1325\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(96390).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Trellis Property rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in commercial real estate are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The founder profile that opens doors — and the one that closes them','Visibility is not the goal. Credibility with a specific audience is.','inherit','closed','closed','','290-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','',290,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1488',0,'revision','',0),(1489,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fatima Al-Rashid publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Nawa Health’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Dubai company sells into digital health, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1326,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-7d34418678-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1326\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Central_business_district%27s_Skyline.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Nawa Health stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fatima Al-Rashid is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for digital health businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Fatima Al-Rashid publishes twice a month. It generates half the pipeline.','A small, consistent output with an unusually high conversion rate.','inherit','closed','closed','','292-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','',292,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1489',0,'revision','',0),(1490,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Kestrel Defence Systems took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Viktor Marchenko describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Warsaw business had a real problem: in aerospace components, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1327,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-61ae1687ee-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1327\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti_2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Kestrel Defence Systems was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Viktor Marchenko regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Executive visibility without the performance','Being useful in public, at a cadence you can actually sustain.','inherit','closed','closed','','294-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','',294,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1490',0,'revision','',0),(1491,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Harbourline Retail could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Grace Nakamura has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Osaka company operates in omnichannel retail, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1328,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2edc6a19f8-1200x641.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1328\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Town_Skyline_captured_from_Bengal_Intelligent_Park,_Saltlake,_Kolkata_(2_of_2_photos).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Subhrajyoti07 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Harbourline Retail rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in omnichannel retail are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','What a credible founder presence looks like now','The bar moved. Volume is no longer mistaken for authority.','inherit','closed','closed','','296-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','',296,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1491',0,'revision','',0),(1492,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Meridian Loop took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Priya Raghunathan describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Singapore business had a real problem: in logistics software, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1329,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8cf0d28ab3-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1329\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DLF_Epitome_Tower,_DLF_Phase_3,_Gurugram.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Sahil Dhiman · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Meridian Loop was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Priya Raghunathan regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Buying committees grew. Meridian Loop rebuilt marketing around that.','Eleven people in the decision, and only one of them ever fills in a form.','inherit','closed','closed','','298-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','',298,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1492',0,'revision','',0),(1493,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Halden Grid could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Daniel Okonkwo has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Lagos company operates in energy infrastructure, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1330,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8ce8266747-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1330\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gurgaon_CyberHub.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Pithwilds · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Halden Grid rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in energy infrastructure are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The pipeline maths most B2B teams get wrong','Working backwards from the number changes what you build first.','inherit','closed','closed','','300-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','',300,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1493',0,'revision','',0),(1494,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Marta Ferreira publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Quorum Health’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Lisbon company sells into clinical operations, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1331,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-a66b67ef58-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1331\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DLF_Cyber_City_with_Rapid_Metro,_Gurgaon.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Quorum Health stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Marta Ferreira is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for clinical operations businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Marta Ferreira on marketing to people who never click','Dark social, private communities, and the demand you cannot see.','inherit','closed','closed','','302-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:46','2026-09-08 04:58:46','',302,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1494',0,'revision','',0),(1495,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Northgate Analytics took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Ethan Brightwell describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Manchester business had a real problem: in risk analytics, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1332,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-dfb8103782-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1332\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ripon_Building_panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">This Image was created by User:PlaneMad.\nIf you are using the image under the cr · CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Northgate Analytics was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Ethan Brightwell regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','ABM works. The version most companies run does not.','Account-based marketing without account-based selling is just expensive advertising.','inherit','closed','closed','','304-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','',304,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1495',0,'revision','',0),(1496,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sana Qureshi publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Tallow & Co.’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Karachi company sells into consumer manufacturing, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1333,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-058579f56b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1333\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chennai_train_station.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arian Zwegers\nderivative work: MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Tallow & Co. stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sana Qureshi is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for consumer manufacturing businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','A point of view is a commitment, not a campaign','Publishing an opinion means defending it when the market moves against you.','inherit','closed','closed','','306-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','',306,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1496',0,'revision','',0),(1497,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Bastion Freight took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Julien Marchetti describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Marseille business had a real problem: in freight forwarding, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1334,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-c7a2f18759-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1334\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_SE_Island_Grounds_Chennai_Jul25_R16_07915.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Timothy A. Gonsalves · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Bastion Freight was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Julien Marchetti regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Julien Marchetti on earning the right to an opinion','Proprietary data, hard-won operating experience, and a willingness to be specific.','inherit','closed','closed','','308-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','',308,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1497',0,'revision','',0),(1498,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Kindred Payments could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Amara Boateng has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Accra company operates in payments, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1335,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-bf7da0674c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1335\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Kindred Payments rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in payments are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The difference between thought leadership and content marketing','One changes what readers believe. The other fills a calendar.','inherit','closed','closed','','310-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','',310,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1498',0,'revision','',0),(1499,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rohan Vaidya publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Sextant Labs’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Pune company sells into developer tooling, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1336,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-d6af6b95ea-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1336\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Awfis_-_Collab_Zone.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Anniefernandes · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Sextant Labs stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Rohan Vaidya is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for developer tooling businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Sextant Labs turned internal research into an industry standard','A benchmark study that competitors now cite in their own marketing.','inherit','closed','closed','','312-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','',312,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1499',0,'revision','',0),(1500,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most consequential communications decision Fjordline Materials made last year was not an announcement. It was the decision to hold one for two quarters, against the advice of almost everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim runs the Bergen business in advanced materials, where announcement cadence is treated as a proxy for momentum. The news in question — a significant partnership — was ready in the spring and did not go out until the autumn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Announcing it in March would have been true. It would also have been the only thing we could say for six months.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1305,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-e1479cc42b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1305\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Earned coverage still starts with a relationship. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_pic_Mumbai.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">webhishek · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Timing as strategy</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reasoning was straightforward once articulated. The partnership was real but not yet operational, and announcing it would have invited exactly one follow-up question that Fjordline Materials could not answer. Held until the first joint customers were live, the same news carried evidence rather than intention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This runs against the instinct of most communications functions, which are measured on volume of coverage rather than its usefulness. The counter-argument Ingrid Solheim makes is that a company gets a limited number of moments where journalists will take its calls, and spending one on an announcement without substance is expensive in a way that does not appear on any report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Announcement timing is a strategic decision, not a scheduling one.</li>\n<li>News without an answer to the obvious follow-up question spends credibility.</li>\n<li>Coverage volume is a poor proxy for communications effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eventual coverage was, by Ingrid Solheim’s account, roughly three times what the spring version would have received, and considerably more accurate. Whether that trade is available to every company is another matter — but it was available here, and almost nobody took it seriously until it worked.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The announcement Fjordline Materials chose not to make','Restraint as strategy: why the news was held for two quarters.','inherit','closed','closed','','314-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','',314,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1500',0,'revision','',0),(1501,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reputation, Caleb Whitfield argues, is the only asset a founder takes with them from one company to the next — and almost everyone starts building it about three years too late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Caleb Whitfield has run Ardent Mutual in Toronto for the better part of a decade and is now, by common consent, the person journalists call first for comment on insurance. That position was not sought so much as accumulated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I answered every question I was asked, accurately, for years, including the ones where the honest answer was that I did not know.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1306,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8336172513-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1306\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Visibility compounds when it is consistent. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_Worli_Sea-Link_(cropped).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">yatriksheth · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>How the position was built</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics were unremarkable: consistent availability, specific answers, and a discipline about staying inside genuine expertise. Caleb Whitfield declined comment on adjacent topics often enough that the acceptances carried weight, and never used an interview to pitch Ardent Mutual — a restraint that reporters noticed and reciprocated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial value is real but indirect. It shows up in inbound partnership conversations, in recruiting, and in the speed with which Ardent Mutual can get a hearing when it does have something to say. It does not show up in any campaign report, which is why it tends to be under-resourced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Declining to comment outside your expertise is what makes your comments valuable.</li>\n<li>Founder reputation is portable in a way company brand is not.</li>\n<li>The return arrives in recruiting and partnerships long before it appears in pipeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Caleb Whitfield is dismissive of the term personal branding and of most of the activity conducted under it. The distinction being drawn is between performing expertise and having it — and, over a decade, that difference is not difficult for anyone to see.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Crisis communications, rehearsed before the crisis','The plan was boring, tested twice a year, and worth every hour.','inherit','closed','closed','','316-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:47','2026-09-08 04:58:47','',316,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1501',0,'revision','',0),(1502,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly had no funding to announce, no customer logos it was permitted to use and no category anyone had named. It got its first significant coverage anyway, and the method is more replicable than it sounds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen founded the Taipei company in industrial robotics, a corner of the market with genuine activity and almost no public data. That absence turned out to be the opening.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Journalists were not going to write about us. They were absolutely going to write about the thing we could measure.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1307,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-94f5b0c663-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1307\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The newsroom decides; the pitch only opens the door. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_03-2016_80_Dadar_Beach_view_of_the_SeaLink.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">A.Savin · FAL</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Data as a pitch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company ran a structured survey of its own market — roughly two hundred responses, methodology published, findings that included several that were unflattering to Orbit Assembly’s own thesis. It was released without embargo, without a press release and with the underlying data available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three publications covered it within a fortnight. Two of them have since come back for comment on unrelated stories, which is the actual return. The product was mentioned once, in a sentence establishing why the company had the data. That was sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Original data is coverable when a product announcement is not.</li>\n<li>Publishing findings that undercut your own positioning buys disproportionate credibility.</li>\n<li>The return on a research piece is the relationships it starts, not the coverage it generates.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen now runs the survey annually and treats it as a fixed cost of the communications function. It remains, by some distance, the cheapest source of earned coverage Orbit Assembly has found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Lin Wei-Chen on talking to journalists who are not writing about you','The relationships that matter are built in the quarters when there is no news.','inherit','closed','closed','','318-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','',318,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1502',0,'revision','',0),(1503,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most consequential communications decision Cellar Six made last year was not an announcement. It was the decision to hold one for two quarters, against the advice of almost everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois runs the Montreal business in food and beverage, where announcement cadence is treated as a proxy for momentum. The news in question — a significant partnership — was ready in the spring and did not go out until the autumn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Announcing it in March would have been true. It would also have been the only thing we could say for six months.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1308,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-820bc1d1ce-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1308\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Earned coverage still starts with a relationship. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumbai_Bandra-Worli_Sea_Link.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Rutiknatekar · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Timing as strategy</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reasoning was straightforward once articulated. The partnership was real but not yet operational, and announcing it would have invited exactly one follow-up question that Cellar Six could not answer. Held until the first joint customers were live, the same news carried evidence rather than intention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This runs against the instinct of most communications functions, which are measured on volume of coverage rather than its usefulness. The counter-argument Nadia Bourgeois makes is that a company gets a limited number of moments where journalists will take its calls, and spending one on an announcement without substance is expensive in a way that does not appear on any report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Announcement timing is a strategic decision, not a scheduling one.</li>\n<li>News without an answer to the obvious follow-up question spends credibility.</li>\n<li>Coverage volume is a poor proxy for communications effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eventual coverage was, by Nadia Bourgeois’s account, roughly three times what the spring version would have received, and considerably more accurate. Whether that trade is available to every company is another matter — but it was available here, and almost nobody took it seriously until it worked.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Corporate messaging that survives contact with reality','If staff cannot repeat it accurately, customers will never hear it.','inherit','closed','closed','','320-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','',320,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1503',0,'revision','',0),(1504,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems had no funding to announce, no customer logos it was permitted to use and no category anyone had named. It got its first significant coverage anyway, and the method is more replicable than it sounds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak founded the Prague company in building automation, a corner of the market with genuine activity and almost no public data. That absence turned out to be the opening.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Journalists were not going to write about us. They were absolutely going to write about the thing we could measure.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1309,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-059b059d92-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1309\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Visibility compounds when it is consistent. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_worli_sealink%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%A6%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B0-%E0%B4%B5%E0%B5%BC%E0%B4%B3%E0%B4%BF_%E0%B4%95%E0%B4%9F%E0%B5%BD%E0%B4%AA%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B2%E0%B4%82.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arjuncm3 · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Data as a pitch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company ran a structured survey of its own market — roughly two hundred responses, methodology published, findings that included several that were unflattering to Vesper Systems’s own thesis. It was released without embargo, without a press release and with the underlying data available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three publications covered it within a fortnight. Two of them have since come back for comment on unrelated stories, which is the actual return. The product was mentioned once, in a sentence establishing why the company had the data. That was sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Original data is coverable when a product announcement is not.</li>\n<li>Publishing findings that undercut your own positioning buys disproportionate credibility.</li>\n<li>The return on a research piece is the relationships it starts, not the coverage it generates.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak now runs the survey annually and treats it as a fixed cost of the communications function. It remains, by some distance, the cheapest source of earned coverage Vesper Systems has found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How to get covered before you have a category','Reporters cannot write about a thing that has no name. Give them the trend instead.','inherit','closed','closed','','322-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','',322,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1504',0,'revision','',0),(1505,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most consequential communications decision Rafiq Capital made last year was not an announcement. It was the decision to hold one for two quarters, against the advice of almost everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour runs the Amman business in small-business lending, where announcement cadence is treated as a proxy for momentum. The news in question — a significant partnership — was ready in the spring and did not go out until the autumn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Announcing it in March would have been true. It would also have been the only thing we could say for six months.</p><cite>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive, Rafiq Capital</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1310,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-1fe5eb45d1-1200x437.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1310\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The newsroom decides; the pitch only opens the door. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangalore_Panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Muhammad Mahdi Karim · GFDL 1.2</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Timing as strategy</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reasoning was straightforward once articulated. The partnership was real but not yet operational, and announcing it would have invited exactly one follow-up question that Rafiq Capital could not answer. Held until the first joint customers were live, the same news carried evidence rather than intention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This runs against the instinct of most communications functions, which are measured on volume of coverage rather than its usefulness. The counter-argument Aisha Mansour makes is that a company gets a limited number of moments where journalists will take its calls, and spending one on an announcement without substance is expensive in a way that does not appear on any report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Announcement timing is a strategic decision, not a scheduling one.</li>\n<li>News without an answer to the obvious follow-up question spends credibility.</li>\n<li>Coverage volume is a poor proxy for communications effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eventual coverage was, by Aisha Mansour’s account, roughly three times what the spring version would have received, and considerably more accurate. Whether that trade is available to every company is another matter — but it was available here, and almost nobody took it seriously until it worked.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Rafiq Capital earned its first coverage without a funding announcement','A dataset, an argument, and three journalists who were already curious.','inherit','closed','closed','','324-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','',324,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1505',0,'revision','',0),(1506,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reputation, Gregor Lindqvist argues, is the only asset a founder takes with them from one company to the next — and almost everyone starts building it about three years too late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Gregor Lindqvist has run Baltic Yield in Malmö for the better part of a decade and is now, by common consent, the person journalists call first for comment on agricultural technology. That position was not sought so much as accumulated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I answered every question I was asked, accurately, for years, including the ones where the honest answer was that I did not know.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1311,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-cc5333e618-1200x437.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1311\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Earned coverage still starts with a relationship. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangalore_Panorama_edit1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Muhammad Mahdi Karim · GFDL 1.2</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>How the position was built</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics were unremarkable: consistent availability, specific answers, and a discipline about staying inside genuine expertise. Gregor Lindqvist declined comment on adjacent topics often enough that the acceptances carried weight, and never used an interview to pitch Baltic Yield — a restraint that reporters noticed and reciprocated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial value is real but indirect. It shows up in inbound partnership conversations, in recruiting, and in the speed with which Baltic Yield can get a hearing when it does have something to say. It does not show up in any campaign report, which is why it tends to be under-resourced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Declining to comment outside your expertise is what makes your comments valuable.</li>\n<li>Founder reputation is portable in a way company brand is not.</li>\n<li>The return arrives in recruiting and partnerships long before it appears in pipeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Gregor Lindqvist is dismissive of the term personal branding and of most of the activity conducted under it. The distinction being drawn is between performing expertise and having it — and, over a decade, that difference is not difficult for anyone to see.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The startup PR budget question, answered honestly','What an agency does, what it cannot do, and when to hire one.','inherit','closed','closed','','326-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','',326,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1506',0,'revision','',0),(1507,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove had no funding to announce, no customer logos it was permitted to use and no category anyone had named. It got its first significant coverage anyway, and the method is more replicable than it sounds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi founded the Milan company in design manufacturing, a corner of the market with genuine activity and almost no public data. That absence turned out to be the opening.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Journalists were not going to write about us. They were absolutely going to write about the thing we could measure.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1312,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-a7f6e6d6a8-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1312\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Visibility compounds when it is consistent. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UB_City_(5392886532).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Saad Faruque from Bangalore, India · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Data as a pitch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company ran a structured survey of its own market — roughly two hundred responses, methodology published, findings that included several that were unflattering to Atelier Nove’s own thesis. It was released without embargo, without a press release and with the underlying data available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three publications covered it within a fortnight. Two of them have since come back for comment on unrelated stories, which is the actual return. The product was mentioned once, in a sentence establishing why the company had the data. That was sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Original data is coverable when a product announcement is not.</li>\n<li>Publishing findings that undercut your own positioning buys disproportionate credibility.</li>\n<li>The return on a research piece is the relationships it starts, not the coverage it generates.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi now runs the survey annually and treats it as a fixed cost of the communications function. It remains, by some distance, the cheapest source of earned coverage Atelier Nove has found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Chiara Bellandi: “We pitched the trend, not the product.”','The product got two sentences. That was enough.','inherit','closed','closed','','328-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:48','2026-09-08 04:58:48','',328,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1507',0,'revision','',0),(1508,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reputation, Samuel Adeyemi argues, is the only asset a founder takes with them from one company to the next — and almost everyone starts building it about three years too late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Samuel Adeyemi has run Longbridge Learning in Nairobi for the better part of a decade and is now, by common consent, the person journalists call first for comment on workforce training. That position was not sought so much as accumulated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I answered every question I was asked, accurately, for years, including the ones where the honest answer was that I did not know.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1313,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-25623f9d0b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1313\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The newsroom decides; the pitch only opens the door. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_Worli_sea_link,_Mumbai.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Rahulransubhe · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>How the position was built</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics were unremarkable: consistent availability, specific answers, and a discipline about staying inside genuine expertise. Samuel Adeyemi declined comment on adjacent topics often enough that the acceptances carried weight, and never used an interview to pitch Longbridge Learning — a restraint that reporters noticed and reciprocated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial value is real but indirect. It shows up in inbound partnership conversations, in recruiting, and in the speed with which Longbridge Learning can get a hearing when it does have something to say. It does not show up in any campaign report, which is why it tends to be under-resourced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Declining to comment outside your expertise is what makes your comments valuable.</li>\n<li>Founder reputation is portable in a way company brand is not.</li>\n<li>The return arrives in recruiting and partnerships long before it appears in pipeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Samuel Adeyemi is dismissive of the term personal branding and of most of the activity conducted under it. The distinction being drawn is between performing expertise and having it — and, over a decade, that difference is not difficult for anyone to see.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Building a founder profile that outlasts one company','Reputation is portable. Most founders build it far too late.','inherit','closed','closed','','330-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','',330,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1508',0,'revision','',0),(1509,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik had no funding to announce, no customer logos it was permitted to use and no category anyone had named. It got its first significant coverage anyway, and the method is more replicable than it sounds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hannah Vogel founded the Hamburg company in warehousing, a corner of the market with genuine activity and almost no public data. That absence turned out to be the opening.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Journalists were not going to write about us. They were absolutely going to write about the thing we could measure.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1314,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-23dc6575c0-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1314\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Earned coverage still starts with a relationship. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandra_Worli_Sea_Link_from_worli_Sea_Face.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Roshan Jain · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Data as a pitch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company ran a structured survey of its own market — roughly two hundred responses, methodology published, findings that included several that were unflattering to Steinweg Logistik’s own thesis. It was released without embargo, without a press release and with the underlying data available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three publications covered it within a fortnight. Two of them have since come back for comment on unrelated stories, which is the actual return. The product was mentioned once, in a sentence establishing why the company had the data. That was sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Original data is coverable when a product announcement is not.</li>\n<li>Publishing findings that undercut your own positioning buys disproportionate credibility.</li>\n<li>The return on a research piece is the relationships it starts, not the coverage it generates.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hannah Vogel now runs the survey annually and treats it as a fixed cost of the communications function. It remains, by some distance, the cheapest source of earned coverage Steinweg Logistik has found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Hannah Vogel became the reference for warehousing. It took three years.','Consistent, specific, and useful — long before anyone was listening.','inherit','closed','closed','','332-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','',332,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1509',0,'revision','',0),(1510,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most consequential communications decision Ribera Foods made last year was not an announcement. It was the decision to hold one for two quarters, against the advice of almost everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo runs the Bogotá business in food distribution, where announcement cadence is treated as a proxy for momentum. The news in question — a significant partnership — was ready in the spring and did not go out until the autumn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Announcing it in March would have been true. It would also have been the only thing we could say for six months.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1315,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0de3464d6c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1315\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Visibility compounds when it is consistent. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_B_Block_at_night,_Delhi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Timing as strategy</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reasoning was straightforward once articulated. The partnership was real but not yet operational, and announcing it would have invited exactly one follow-up question that Ribera Foods could not answer. Held until the first joint customers were live, the same news carried evidence rather than intention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This runs against the instinct of most communications functions, which are measured on volume of coverage rather than its usefulness. The counter-argument Diego Salcedo makes is that a company gets a limited number of moments where journalists will take its calls, and spending one on an announcement without substance is expensive in a way that does not appear on any report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Announcement timing is a strategic decision, not a scheduling one.</li>\n<li>News without an answer to the obvious follow-up question spends credibility.</li>\n<li>Coverage volume is a poor proxy for communications effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eventual coverage was, by Diego Salcedo’s account, roughly three times what the spring version would have received, and considerably more accurate. Whether that trade is available to every company is another matter — but it was available here, and almost nobody took it seriously until it worked.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The founder interview, prepared properly','What to bring, what to concede, and the three things never to say.','inherit','closed','closed','','334-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','',334,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1510',0,'revision','',0),(1511,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reputation, Meera Krishnan argues, is the only asset a founder takes with them from one company to the next — and almost everyone starts building it about three years too late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meera Krishnan has run Aster Fieldworks in Chennai for the better part of a decade and is now, by common consent, the person journalists call first for comment on field service software. That position was not sought so much as accumulated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I answered every question I was asked, accurately, for years, including the ones where the honest answer was that I did not know.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1316,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0d4f7b61eb-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1316\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The newsroom decides; the pitch only opens the door. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_New_Delhi_at_night.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>How the position was built</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics were unremarkable: consistent availability, specific answers, and a discipline about staying inside genuine expertise. Meera Krishnan declined comment on adjacent topics often enough that the acceptances carried weight, and never used an interview to pitch Aster Fieldworks — a restraint that reporters noticed and reciprocated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial value is real but indirect. It shows up in inbound partnership conversations, in recruiting, and in the speed with which Aster Fieldworks can get a hearing when it does have something to say. It does not show up in any campaign report, which is why it tends to be under-resourced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Declining to comment outside your expertise is what makes your comments valuable.</li>\n<li>Founder reputation is portable in a way company brand is not.</li>\n<li>The return arrives in recruiting and partnerships long before it appears in pipeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meera Krishnan is dismissive of the term personal branding and of most of the activity conducted under it. The distinction being drawn is between performing expertise and having it — and, over a decade, that difference is not difficult for anyone to see.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Visibility compounds — if it is consistent','Twelve mediocre appearances beat one excellent appearance, most of the time.','inherit','closed','closed','','336-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','',336,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1511',0,'revision','',0),(1512,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most consequential communications decision Trellis Property made last year was not an announcement. It was the decision to hold one for two quarters, against the advice of almost everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth runs the Sydney business in commercial real estate, where announcement cadence is treated as a proxy for momentum. The news in question — a significant partnership — was ready in the spring and did not go out until the autumn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Announcing it in March would have been true. It would also have been the only thing we could say for six months.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Earned coverage still starts with a relationship. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place_sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ville Miettinen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Timing as strategy</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reasoning was straightforward once articulated. The partnership was real but not yet operational, and announcing it would have invited exactly one follow-up question that Trellis Property could not answer. Held until the first joint customers were live, the same news carried evidence rather than intention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This runs against the instinct of most communications functions, which are measured on volume of coverage rather than its usefulness. The counter-argument Oliver Ashworth makes is that a company gets a limited number of moments where journalists will take its calls, and spending one on an announcement without substance is expensive in a way that does not appear on any report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Announcement timing is a strategic decision, not a scheduling one.</li>\n<li>News without an answer to the obvious follow-up question spends credibility.</li>\n<li>Coverage volume is a poor proxy for communications effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eventual coverage was, by Oliver Ashworth’s account, roughly three times what the spring version would have received, and considerably more accurate. Whether that trade is available to every company is another matter — but it was available here, and almost nobody took it seriously until it worked.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Who covered what: commercial real estate in the press this quarter','A read of the coverage, the angles and the reporters driving them.','inherit','closed','closed','','338-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','',338,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1512',0,'revision','',0),(1513,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reputation, Fatima Al-Rashid argues, is the only asset a founder takes with them from one company to the next — and almost everyone starts building it about three years too late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fatima Al-Rashid has run Nawa Health in Dubai for the better part of a decade and is now, by common consent, the person journalists call first for comment on digital health. That position was not sought so much as accumulated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I answered every question I was asked, accurately, for years, including the ones where the honest answer was that I did not know.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Visibility compounds when it is consistent. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(26528).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>How the position was built</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics were unremarkable: consistent availability, specific answers, and a discipline about staying inside genuine expertise. Fatima Al-Rashid declined comment on adjacent topics often enough that the acceptances carried weight, and never used an interview to pitch Nawa Health — a restraint that reporters noticed and reciprocated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial value is real but indirect. It shows up in inbound partnership conversations, in recruiting, and in the speed with which Nawa Health can get a hearing when it does have something to say. It does not show up in any campaign report, which is why it tends to be under-resourced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Declining to comment outside your expertise is what makes your comments valuable.</li>\n<li>Founder reputation is portable in a way company brand is not.</li>\n<li>The return arrives in recruiting and partnerships long before it appears in pipeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fatima Al-Rashid is dismissive of the term personal branding and of most of the activity conducted under it. The distinction being drawn is between performing expertise and having it — and, over a decade, that difference is not difficult for anyone to see.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Nawa Health’s coverage spike, explained','One story, syndicated eleven times, and what it did to inbound.','inherit','closed','closed','','340-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','',340,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1513',0,'revision','',0),(1514,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Kestrel Defence Systems had no funding to announce, no customer logos it was permitted to use and no category anyone had named. It got its first significant coverage anyway, and the method is more replicable than it sounds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko founded the Warsaw company in aerospace components, a corner of the market with genuine activity and almost no public data. That absence turned out to be the opening.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Journalists were not going to write about us. They were absolutely going to write about the thing we could measure.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The newsroom decides; the pitch only opens the door. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(37550).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Data as a pitch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company ran a structured survey of its own market — roughly two hundred responses, methodology published, findings that included several that were unflattering to Kestrel Defence Systems’s own thesis. It was released without embargo, without a press release and with the underlying data available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three publications covered it within a fortnight. Two of them have since come back for comment on unrelated stories, which is the actual return. The product was mentioned once, in a sentence establishing why the company had the data. That was sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Original data is coverable when a product announcement is not.</li>\n<li>Publishing findings that undercut your own positioning buys disproportionate credibility.</li>\n<li>The return on a research piece is the relationships it starts, not the coverage it generates.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko now runs the survey annually and treats it as a fixed cost of the communications function. It remains, by some distance, the cheapest source of earned coverage Kestrel Defence Systems has found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The stories editors are actually commissioning','What is getting through, straight from the desks deciding.','inherit','closed','closed','','342-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:49','2026-09-08 04:58:49','',342,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1514',0,'revision','',0),(1515,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most consequential communications decision Harbourline Retail made last year was not an announcement. It was the decision to hold one for two quarters, against the advice of almost everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura runs the Osaka business in omnichannel retail, where announcement cadence is treated as a proxy for momentum. The news in question — a significant partnership — was ready in the spring and did not go out until the autumn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Announcing it in March would have been true. It would also have been the only thing we could say for six months.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Earned coverage still starts with a relationship. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(47183).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Timing as strategy</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reasoning was straightforward once articulated. The partnership was real but not yet operational, and announcing it would have invited exactly one follow-up question that Harbourline Retail could not answer. Held until the first joint customers were live, the same news carried evidence rather than intention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This runs against the instinct of most communications functions, which are measured on volume of coverage rather than its usefulness. The counter-argument Grace Nakamura makes is that a company gets a limited number of moments where journalists will take its calls, and spending one on an announcement without substance is expensive in a way that does not appear on any report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Announcement timing is a strategic decision, not a scheduling one.</li>\n<li>News without an answer to the obvious follow-up question spends credibility.</li>\n<li>Coverage volume is a poor proxy for communications effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eventual coverage was, by Grace Nakamura’s account, roughly three times what the spring version would have received, and considerably more accurate. Whether that trade is available to every company is another matter — but it was available here, and almost nobody took it seriously until it worked.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Coverage without a news hook: how Harbourline Retail did it','Original data beats an announcement, almost every time.','inherit','closed','closed','','344-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','',344,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1515',0,'revision','',0),(1516,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop had no funding to announce, no customer logos it was permitted to use and no category anyone had named. It got its first significant coverage anyway, and the method is more replicable than it sounds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan founded the Singapore company in logistics software, a corner of the market with genuine activity and almost no public data. That absence turned out to be the opening.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Journalists were not going to write about us. They were absolutely going to write about the thing we could measure.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Visibility compounds when it is consistent. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(80844).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Data as a pitch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company ran a structured survey of its own market — roughly two hundred responses, methodology published, findings that included several that were unflattering to Meridian Loop’s own thesis. It was released without embargo, without a press release and with the underlying data available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three publications covered it within a fortnight. Two of them have since come back for comment on unrelated stories, which is the actual return. The product was mentioned once, in a sentence establishing why the company had the data. That was sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Original data is coverable when a product announcement is not.</li>\n<li>Publishing findings that undercut your own positioning buys disproportionate credibility.</li>\n<li>The return on a research piece is the relationships it starts, not the coverage it generates.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan now runs the survey annually and treats it as a fixed cost of the communications function. It remains, by some distance, the cheapest source of earned coverage Meridian Loop has found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Awards, speaking and the mechanics of being known','What the visibility circuit actually returns, measured honestly.','inherit','closed','closed','','346-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','',346,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1516',0,'revision','',0),(1517,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The most consequential communications decision Halden Grid made last year was not an announcement. It was the decision to hold one for two quarters, against the advice of almost everyone involved.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo runs the Lagos business in energy infrastructure, where announcement cadence is treated as a proxy for momentum. The news in question — a significant partnership — was ready in the spring and did not go out until the autumn.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Announcing it in March would have been true. It would also have been the only thing we could say for six months.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1322,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-d46d8e78c5-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1322\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The newsroom decides; the pitch only opens the door. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City_buildings_(30087).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Timing as strategy</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The reasoning was straightforward once articulated. The partnership was real but not yet operational, and announcing it would have invited exactly one follow-up question that Halden Grid could not answer. Held until the first joint customers were live, the same news carried evidence rather than intention.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>This runs against the instinct of most communications functions, which are measured on volume of coverage rather than its usefulness. The counter-argument Daniel Okonkwo makes is that a company gets a limited number of moments where journalists will take its calls, and spending one on an announcement without substance is expensive in a way that does not appear on any report.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Announcement timing is a strategic decision, not a scheduling one.</li>\n<li>News without an answer to the obvious follow-up question spends credibility.</li>\n<li>Coverage volume is a poor proxy for communications effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The eventual coverage was, by Daniel Okonkwo’s account, roughly three times what the spring version would have received, and considerably more accurate. Whether that trade is available to every company is another matter — but it was available here, and almost nobody took it seriously until it worked.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Halden Grid skipped the conference circuit. Here is what it did instead.','Twelve dinners, one report, and a much better pipeline.','inherit','closed','closed','','348-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','',348,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1517',0,'revision','',0),(1518,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Reputation, Marta Ferreira argues, is the only asset a founder takes with them from one company to the next — and almost everyone starts building it about three years too late.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Marta Ferreira has run Quorum Health in Lisbon for the better part of a decade and is now, by common consent, the person journalists call first for comment on clinical operations. That position was not sought so much as accumulated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I answered every question I was asked, accurately, for years, including the ones where the honest answer was that I did not know.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Earned coverage still starts with a relationship. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(98608).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>How the position was built</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The mechanics were unremarkable: consistent availability, specific answers, and a discipline about staying inside genuine expertise. Marta Ferreira declined comment on adjacent topics often enough that the acceptances carried weight, and never used an interview to pitch Quorum Health — a restraint that reporters noticed and reciprocated.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial value is real but indirect. It shows up in inbound partnership conversations, in recruiting, and in the speed with which Quorum Health can get a hearing when it does have something to say. It does not show up in any campaign report, which is why it tends to be under-resourced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Declining to comment outside your expertise is what makes your comments valuable.</li>\n<li>Founder reputation is portable in a way company brand is not.</li>\n<li>The return arrives in recruiting and partnerships long before it appears in pipeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Marta Ferreira is dismissive of the term personal branding and of most of the activity conducted under it. The distinction being drawn is between performing expertise and having it — and, over a decade, that difference is not difficult for anyone to see.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The publicity plan that fits a twenty-person company','Modest budget, narrow audience, and no wasted motion.','inherit','closed','closed','','350-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','',350,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1518',0,'revision','',0),(1519,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics had no funding to announce, no customer logos it was permitted to use and no category anyone had named. It got its first significant coverage anyway, and the method is more replicable than it sounds.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell founded the Manchester company in risk analytics, a corner of the market with genuine activity and almost no public data. That absence turned out to be the opening.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Journalists were not going to write about us. They were absolutely going to write about the thing we could measure.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1324,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ae876be62e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1324\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Visibility compounds when it is consistent. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemon_Tree_hotel_in_HITEC_city.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Data as a pitch</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The company ran a structured survey of its own market — roughly two hundred responses, methodology published, findings that included several that were unflattering to Northgate Analytics’s own thesis. It was released without embargo, without a press release and with the underlying data available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Three publications covered it within a fortnight. Two of them have since come back for comment on unrelated stories, which is the actual return. The product was mentioned once, in a sentence establishing why the company had the data. That was sufficient.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Original data is coverable when a product announcement is not.</li>\n<li>Publishing findings that undercut your own positioning buys disproportionate credibility.</li>\n<li>The return on a research piece is the relationships it starts, not the coverage it generates.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell now runs the survey annually and treats it as a fixed cost of the communications function. It remains, by some distance, the cheapest source of earned coverage Northgate Analytics has found.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Ethan Brightwell on choosing the three rooms that matter','Being everywhere is a strategy for companies with nothing to say.','inherit','closed','closed','','352-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','',352,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1519',0,'revision','',0),(1520,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Sana Qureshi where the story stops being about Tallow & Co. and starts being about consumer manufacturing itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. was built in Karachi on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Sana Qureshi spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">From the cover feature in this month’s edition. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place_sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ville Miettinen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in consumer manufacturing depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow & Co. did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Sana Qureshi anticipated takes another five years, Tallow & Co. has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Sana Qureshi is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in consumer manufacturing have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Sana Qureshi’s timeline is the one variable Tallow & Co. cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Cover story: Sana Qureshi, Tallow & Co.','Our cover profile on the consumer manufacturing operator building for the long horizon.','inherit','closed','closed','','354-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:50','2026-09-08 04:58:50','',354,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1520',0,'revision','',0),(1521,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:51','2026-09-08 04:58:51','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Julien Marchetti what Bastion Freight does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Julien Marchetti’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight operates in freight forwarding from Marseille with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Photographed for Global Media Star. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(26528).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Bastion Freight lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Julien Marchetti spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Julien Marchetti joins the GMS cover list','Recognised for the work of building a durable business in Marseille.','inherit','closed','closed','','356-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:51','2026-09-08 04:58:51','',356,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1521',0,'revision','',0),(1522,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:51','2026-09-08 04:58:51','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng does not do many interviews. The Accra office has no press page, Kindred Payments has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Amara Boateng laid out how Kindred Payments came to occupy the position it holds in payments: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">One of the entrepreneurs on our cover list. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(37550).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in payments were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Kindred Payments did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Kindred Payments is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Kindred Payments navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The entrepreneurs on our covers this year','A look back at the founders and chief executives we profiled.','inherit','closed','closed','','358-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:51','2026-09-08 04:58:51','',358,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1522',0,'revision','',0),(1523,0,'2026-09-08 10:28:51','2026-09-08 04:58:51','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Rohan Vaidya where the story stops being about Sextant Labs and starts being about developer tooling itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs was built in Pune on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Rohan Vaidya spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">From the cover feature in this month’s edition. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(47183).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in developer tooling depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Rohan Vaidya anticipated takes another five years, Sextant Labs has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Rohan Vaidya is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in developer tooling have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Rohan Vaidya’s timeline is the one variable Sextant Labs cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Featured: Rohan Vaidya on building Sextant Labs the slow way','No shortcuts, no hype cycle, and a company that will outlast both.','inherit','closed','closed','','360-revision-v1','','','2026-09-08 10:28:51','2026-09-08 04:58:51','',360,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1523',0,'revision','',0),(1552,0,'2026-09-08 11:13:32','2026-09-08 05:43:32','','Global Media Star — reversed','','inherit','open','closed','','global-media-star-reversed','','','2026-09-08 11:13:32','2026-09-08 05:43:32','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/global-media-star-logo-dark.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),(1554,1,'2026-09-08 11:47:11','2026-09-08 06:17:11','jjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjjj','Screenshot from 2026-08-23 12-48-53','ss','inherit','open','closed','','screenshot-from-2026-08-23-12-48-53','','','2026-09-08 11:47:49','2026-09-08 06:17:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Screenshot-from-2026-08-23-12-48-53.png',0,'attachment','image/png',0),(1556,1,'2026-09-08 12:27:15','2026-09-08 06:57:15','{\"version\": 3, \"isGlobalStylesUserThemeJSON\": true }','Custom Styles','','publish','closed','closed','','wp-global-styles-cbw-magazine','','','2026-09-08 12:27:15','2026-09-08 06:57:15','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-global-styles-cbw-magazine/',0,'wp_global_styles','',0),(1574,0,'2026-09-14 12:13:19','2026-09-14 06:43:19','','Eleanor Hartley','','inherit','open','closed','','eleanor-hartley-2','','','2026-09-14 12:13:19','2026-09-14 06:43:19','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/av-e-hartley.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1575,0,'2026-09-14 12:13:19','2026-09-14 06:43:19','','Marcus De Souza','','inherit','open','closed','','marcus-de-souza-2','','','2026-09-14 12:13:19','2026-09-14 06:43:19','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/av-m-desouza.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1576,0,'2026-09-14 12:13:20','2026-09-14 06:43:20','','Ayesha Iqbal','','inherit','open','closed','','ayesha-iqbal-2','','','2026-09-14 12:13:20','2026-09-14 06:43:20','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/av-a-iqbal.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1577,0,'2026-09-14 12:13:21','2026-09-14 06:43:21','','Joseph Linton','','inherit','open','closed','','joseph-linton-2','','','2026-09-14 12:13:21','2026-09-14 06:43:21','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/av-j-linton.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1578,0,'2026-09-14 12:13:22','2026-09-14 06:43:22','','Sipho Mbeki','','inherit','open','closed','','sipho-mbeki-2','','','2026-09-14 12:13:22','2026-09-14 06:43:22','',0,'http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/av-s-mbeki.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),(1582,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:38','2026-10-12 10:50:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Hannah Vogel what Steinweg Logistik does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Hannah Vogel’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Steinweg Logistik operates in warehousing from Hamburg with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Hannah Vogel, chief executive, Steinweg Logistik</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1316,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0d4f7b61eb-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1316\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_New_Delhi_at_night.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Steinweg Logistik lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Hannah Vogel spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Hannah Vogel is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Ten years, two near-deaths and one very good idea','The long version of the Steinweg Logistik story, told by the person who lived it.','inherit','closed','closed','','188-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:38','2026-10-12 10:50:38','',188,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1582',0,'revision','',0),(1583,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:38','2026-10-12 10:50:38','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Fatima Al-Rashid where the story stops being about Nawa Health and starts being about digital health itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health was built in Dubai on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Fatima Al-Rashid spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(47183).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in digital health depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nawa Health did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Fatima Al-Rashid anticipated takes another five years, Nawa Health has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Fatima Al-Rashid is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in digital health have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Fatima Al-Rashid’s timeline is the one variable Nawa Health cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The chief executive who put engineers in front of customers','It broke the support process, then fixed the product.','inherit','closed','closed','','196-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:38','2026-10-12 10:50:38','',196,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1583',0,'revision','',0),(1584,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim does not do many interviews. The Bergen office has no press page, Fjordline Materials has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Ingrid Solheim laid out how Fjordline Materials came to occupy the position it holds in advanced materials: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Ingrid Solheim, founder, Fjordline Materials</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1331,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-a66b67ef58-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1331\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DLF_Cyber_City_with_Rapid_Metro,_Gurgaon.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in advanced materials were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Fjordline Materials did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ingrid Solheim is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Fjordline Materials is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Fjordline Materials navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Spotlight: Ingrid Solheim, Fjordline Materials','Our editors on why this founder is worth watching in advanced materials.','inherit','closed','closed','','218-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','',218,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1584',0,'revision','',0),(1585,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Samuel Adeyemi where the story stops being about Longbridge Learning and starts being about workforce training itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Longbridge Learning was built in Nairobi on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Samuel Adeyemi spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1315,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-0de3464d6c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1315\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place,_B_Block_at_night,_Delhi.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Slyronit · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in workforce training depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Longbridge Learning did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Samuel Adeyemi anticipated takes another five years, Longbridge Learning has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Samuel Adeyemi is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in workforce training have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Samuel Adeyemi’s timeline is the one variable Longbridge Learning cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Samuel Adeyemi: the operator quietly rewriting workforce training','Little press, no conference circuit, and a business that keeps taking share.','inherit','closed','closed','','186-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','',186,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1585',0,'revision','',0),(1586,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo does not do many interviews. The Bogotá office has no press page, Ribera Foods has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Diego Salcedo laid out how Ribera Foods came to occupy the position it holds in food distribution: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place_sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ville Miettinen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in food distribution were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Ribera Foods did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Ribera Foods is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Ribera Foods navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Diego Salcedo is building Ribera Foods for a market that does not exist yet','A bet on where food distribution lands in five years — and a plan to survive until it does.','inherit','closed','closed','','190-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:39','2026-10-12 10:50:39','',190,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1586',0,'revision','',0),(1587,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:40','2026-10-12 10:50:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Marta Ferreira where the story stops being about Quorum Health and starts being about clinical operations itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health was built in Lisbon on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Marta Ferreira spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Marta Ferreira, chief executive, Quorum Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1325,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-60852483f6-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1325\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(96390).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in clinical operations depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Quorum Health did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Marta Ferreira anticipated takes another five years, Quorum Health has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Marta Ferreira is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in clinical operations have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Marta Ferreira’s timeline is the one variable Quorum Health cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Quorum Health’s bet on doing less, better','Half the product line, twice the attachment rate, and a much simpler company.','inherit','closed','closed','','206-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:40','2026-10-12 10:50:40','',206,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1587',0,'revision','',0),(1588,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:40','2026-10-12 10:50:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Ethan Brightwell what Northgate Analytics does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Ethan Brightwell’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northgate Analytics operates in risk analytics from Manchester with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1326,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-7d34418678-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1326\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Central_business_district%27s_Skyline.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Northgate Analytics lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Ethan Brightwell spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ethan Brightwell is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Anatomy of a turnaround: Northgate Analytics, four years on','What actually worked, what was luck, and what the management team would repeat.','inherit','closed','closed','','208-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:40','2026-10-12 10:50:40','',208,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1588',0,'revision','',0),(1589,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:40','2026-10-12 10:50:40','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Priya Raghunathan what Meridian Loop does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Priya Raghunathan’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meridian Loop operates in logistics software from Singapore with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(98608).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Meridian Loop lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Priya Raghunathan spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raghunathan is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The quiet reinvention of Meridian Loop','No rebrand, no relaunch — just a different business at the end of it.','inherit','closed','closed','','202-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:40','2026-10-12 10:50:40','',202,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1589',0,'revision','',0),(1590,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois does not do many interviews. The Montreal office has no press page, Cellar Six has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Nadia Bourgeois laid out how Cellar Six came to occupy the position it holds in food and beverage: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Nadia Bourgeois, founder, Cellar Six</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1334,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-c7a2f18759-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1334\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_SE_Island_Grounds_Chennai_Jul25_R16_07915.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Timothy A. Gonsalves · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in food and beverage were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Cellar Six did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Nadia Bourgeois is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Cellar Six is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Cellar Six navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The entrepreneur our editors are watching: Nadia Bourgeois','Building steadily in Montreal, in a category the market has not priced yet.','inherit','closed','closed','','224-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','',224,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1590',0,'revision','',0),(1591,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Gregor Lindqvist where the story stops being about Baltic Yield and starts being about agricultural technology itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield was built in Malmö on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Gregor Lindqvist spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1337,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-83860a1e13-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1337\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BHIVE_Workspace_-_HSR_Bangalore.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Coworker2016 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in agricultural technology depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Baltic Yield did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Gregor Lindqvist anticipated takes another five years, Baltic Yield has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Gregor Lindqvist is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in agricultural technology have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Gregor Lindqvist’s timeline is the one variable Baltic Yield cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Baltic Yield at a glance: the numbers behind the narrative','Revenue mix, retention, headcount and the trajectory they imply.','inherit','closed','closed','','230-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','',230,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1591',0,'revision','',0),(1592,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth does not do many interviews. The Sydney office has no press page, Trellis Property has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Oliver Ashworth laid out how Trellis Property came to occupy the position it holds in commercial real estate: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(37550).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in commercial real estate were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Trellis Property did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Trellis Property is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Trellis Property navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Oliver Ashworth, chief executive of Trellis Property, on scale without sprawl','Growing revenue faster than complexity is the whole job, and almost nobody does it.','inherit','closed','closed','','194-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','',194,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1592',0,'revision','',0),(1593,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Chiara Bellandi what Atelier Nove does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Chiara Bellandi’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Atelier Nove operates in design manufacturing from Milan with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1338,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-31e0fe1f5d-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1338\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guest_seating_and_planter_boxes_inside_an_office,_Filmnagar_(1).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Atelier Nove lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Chiara Bellandi spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Chiara Bellandi is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Profile: how Atelier Nove makes money in design manufacturing','Three revenue lines, one of which is quietly becoming the business.','inherit','closed','closed','','232-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:41','2026-10-12 10:50:41','',232,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1593',0,'revision','',0),(1594,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:42','2026-10-12 10:50:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Sana Qureshi where the story stops being about Tallow &amp; Co. and starts being about consumer manufacturing itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow &amp; Co. was built in Karachi on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Sana Qureshi spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Sana Qureshi, founder and chief executive, Tallow &amp; Co.</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1327,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-61ae1687ee-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1327\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti_2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in consumer manufacturing depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tallow &amp; Co. did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Sana Qureshi anticipated takes another five years, Tallow &amp; Co. has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Sana Qureshi is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in consumer manufacturing have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Sana Qureshi’s timeline is the one variable Tallow &amp; Co. cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Tallow &amp; Co. is small, unfashionable and growing fast','Twelve people, no category, and a waiting list of enterprise buyers.','inherit','closed','closed','','210-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:42','2026-10-12 10:50:42','',210,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1594',0,'revision','',0),(1595,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:42','2026-10-12 10:50:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Viktor Marchenko what Kestrel Defence Systems does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Viktor Marchenko’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Kestrel Defence Systems operates in aerospace components from Warsaw with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(80844).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Kestrel Defence Systems lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Viktor Marchenko spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Viktor Marchenko is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Viktor Marchenko: “The job is choosing what not to do.”','A long conversation about focus, capital allocation and saying no in public.','inherit','closed','closed','','198-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:42','2026-10-12 10:50:42','',198,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1595',0,'revision','',0),(1596,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:42','2026-10-12 10:50:42','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour does not do many interviews. The Amman office has no press page, Rafiq Capital has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Aisha Mansour laid out how Rafiq Capital came to occupy the position it holds in small-business lending: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Aisha Mansour, founder and chief executive, Rafiq Capital</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1336,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-workspace-d6af6b95ea-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Office and workspace in India\" class=\"wp-image-1336\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Awfis_-_Collab_Zone.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Anniefernandes · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in small-business lending were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Rafiq Capital did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aisha Mansour is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Rafiq Capital is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Rafiq Capital navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','What Rafiq Capital sells — and to whom','A clear-eyed look at the customer base behind the growth rate.','inherit','closed','closed','','228-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:42','2026-10-12 10:50:42','',228,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1596',0,'revision','',0),(1597,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:43','2026-10-12 10:50:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Meera Krishnan where the story stops being about Aster Fieldworks and starts being about field service software itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aster Fieldworks was built in Chennai on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Meera Krishnan spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1318,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ad11ab591f-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1318\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(26528).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in field service software depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Aster Fieldworks did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Meera Krishnan anticipated takes another five years, Aster Fieldworks has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Meera Krishnan is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in field service software have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Meera Krishnan’s timeline is the one variable Aster Fieldworks cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The founder who made patience a strategy','Slow hiring, slow fundraising, and a compounding rate most of the market missed.','inherit','closed','closed','','192-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:43','2026-10-12 10:50:43','',192,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1597',0,'revision','',0),(1598,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:43','2026-10-12 10:50:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng does not do many interviews. The Accra office has no press page, Kindred Payments has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Amara Boateng laid out how Kindred Payments came to occupy the position it holds in payments: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Amara Boateng, chief executive, Kindred Payments</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1329,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8cf0d28ab3-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1329\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DLF_Epitome_Tower,_DLF_Phase_3,_Gurugram.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Sahil Dhiman · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in payments were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Kindred Payments did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Amara Boateng is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Kindred Payments is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Kindred Payments navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Kindred Payments raised late, hired slowly and won anyway','A counter-cyclical playbook, executed with unusual patience.','inherit','closed','closed','','214-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:43','2026-10-12 10:50:43','',214,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1598',0,'revision','',0),(1599,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:43','2026-10-12 10:50:43','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo does not do many interviews. The Lagos office has no press page, Halden Grid has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Daniel Okonkwo laid out how Halden Grid came to occupy the position it holds in energy infrastructure: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1324,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ae876be62e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1324\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemon_Tree_hotel_in_HITEC_city.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in energy infrastructure were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Halden Grid did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Daniel Okonkwo is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Halden Grid is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Halden Grid navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How a energy infrastructure company became an infrastructure business','The product other companies build on top of, and the margin that comes with it.','inherit','closed','closed','','204-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:43','2026-10-12 10:50:43','',204,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1599',0,'revision','',0),(1600,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:44','2026-10-12 10:50:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Julien Marchetti what Bastion Freight does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Julien Marchetti’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Bastion Freight operates in freight forwarding from Marseille with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Julien Marchetti, managing director, Bastion Freight</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1328,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2edc6a19f8-1200x641.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1328\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Town_Skyline_captured_from_Bengal_Intelligent_Park,_Saltlake,_Kolkata_(2_of_2_photos).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Subhrajyoti07 · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Bastion Freight lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Julien Marchetti spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Julien Marchetti is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The seed-stage company enterprise buyers keep calling','Procurement departments are not supposed to find you this early.','inherit','closed','closed','','212-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:44','2026-10-12 10:50:44','',212,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1600',0,'revision','',0),(1601,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:44','2026-10-12 10:50:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura does not do many interviews. The Osaka office has no press page, Harbourline Retail has never issued a funding announcement, and the company’s most significant product decision of the past three years was communicated to customers in an email of four paragraphs. This is, it turns out, entirely deliberate.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Over two conversations, Grace Nakamura laid out how Harbourline Retail came to occupy the position it holds in omnichannel retail: not through a breakthrough, but through a decade of compounding decisions in a category most investors considered structurally unattractive.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Everyone was looking for the part of this market that could be disrupted. We were much more interested in the part that could be operated properly.</p><cite>Grace Nakamura, chief executive, Harbourline Retail</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1322,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-d46d8e78c5-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1322\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">A closer look at the company behind the headline. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City_buildings_(30087).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The unglamorous thesis</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The founding insight was that the incumbents in omnichannel retail were not badly run so much as badly configured. They had been assembled through acquisition, carried four overlapping systems, and could not answer basic questions about their own customers without a two-week data project. Harbourline Retail did not build a better product first. It built a better view of the customer, and then built product against it.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>That sequencing shows up everywhere in the business. Pricing is set from usage data the competition does not collect. Support is staffed against a churn model rather than a ticket queue. Even the sales compensation plan is derived from retention cohorts. None of it is visible from outside, which is precisely why it has taken competitors so long to respond.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What comes next</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Grace Nakamura is candid that the next phase is harder. The advantages that come from operating well are real but bounded, and Harbourline Retail is now large enough that its own systems are starting to show the seams. A significant replatforming is underway, deliberately spread across three years to avoid the failure mode that has claimed several competitors.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Operating advantage compounds quietly and is difficult for competitors to observe.</li>\n<li>Building the customer view before the product changes every subsequent decision.</li>\n<li>The failure mode of a successful operator is usually its own accumulated systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Whether Harbourline Retail navigates that transition is the question our editors will be watching. But the record so far suggests a company that has been consistently underestimated by people looking for a more exciting story than the one available.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside Grace Nakamura’s three-year plan for Harbourline Retail','Three priorities, one number each, and an unusual amount of specificity.','inherit','closed','closed','','200-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:44','2026-10-12 10:50:44','',200,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1601',0,'revision','',0),(1602,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:44','2026-10-12 10:50:44','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Rohan Vaidya where the story stops being about Sextant Labs and starts being about developer tooling itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs was built in Pune on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Rohan Vaidya spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Rohan Vaidya, co-founder, Sextant Labs</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1330,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8ce8266747-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1330\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gurgaon_CyberHub.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Pithwilds · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in developer tooling depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sextant Labs did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Rohan Vaidya anticipated takes another five years, Sextant Labs has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Rohan Vaidya is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in developer tooling have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Rohan Vaidya’s timeline is the one variable Sextant Labs cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Inside the developer tooling startup rewriting a thirty-year-old workflow','The incumbents are not wrong about the problem. They are wrong about the shape of the answer.','inherit','closed','closed','','216-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:44','2026-10-12 10:50:44','',216,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1602',0,'revision','',0),(1603,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:45','2026-10-12 10:50:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Tomas Novak what Vesper Systems does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Tomas Novak’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesper Systems operates in building automation from Prague with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1335,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-bf7da0674c-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1335\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Vesper Systems lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Tomas Novak spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Tomas Novak is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Vesper Systems, profiled: model, margins and momentum','What the business actually is, underneath the positioning.','inherit','closed','closed','','226-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:45','2026-10-12 10:50:45','',226,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1603',0,'revision','',0),(1604,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:45','2026-10-12 10:50:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>There is a moment in every conversation with Caleb Whitfield where the story stops being about Ardent Mutual and starts being about insurance itself. It usually arrives about twenty minutes in, and it is the part worth staying for.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual was built in Toronto on a bet that a workflow largely unchanged since the 1990s was about to become economically indefensible. The bet was placed early enough that Caleb Whitfield spent three years explaining it to people who were, quite reasonably, unconvinced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Being early and being wrong feel exactly the same from the inside. The only difference is what happens afterwards.</p><cite>Caleb Whitfield, chief executive, Ardent Mutual</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1332,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-dfb8103782-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1332\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">The detail that only shows up when you visit. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ripon_Building_panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">This Image was created by User:PlaneMad.\nIf you are using the image under the cr · CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The bet, explained</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The argument is structural rather than technological. The incumbent workflow in insurance depends on a class of specialist labour that is retiring faster than it is being replaced, and on a tolerance for manual reconciliation that customers of a certain generation simply no longer have. Neither trend is reversible, and neither is being priced.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ardent Mutual did not attempt to replace the workflow wholesale — an approach that had already claimed two better-funded competitors. It replaced a single step, the one that generated the most rework, and let customers keep everything else. Expansion came later, and came from the customers rather than the sales team.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What our editors are watching</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The risk now is timing rather than thesis. If the transition Caleb Whitfield anticipated takes another five years, Ardent Mutual has the balance sheet to wait; if a large incumbent decides to build rather than buy, the window narrows considerably. Caleb Whitfield is unusually relaxed about the second scenario, on the grounds that incumbents in insurance have a long record of choosing not to.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Structural bets on labour and tolerance often outperform bets on technology.</li>\n<li>Replacing one step of a workflow beats replacing all of it, almost every time.</li>\n<li>Expansion led by customers is slower and considerably more durable than expansion led by sales.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>It is a patient thesis, held by a founder with the temperament for it. Whether the market cooperates on Caleb Whitfield’s timeline is the one variable Ardent Mutual cannot control — and the one that will decide the story.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Five questions with Caleb Whitfield','On capital, hiring, competition and the decision that still keeps them up.','inherit','closed','closed','','220-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:45','2026-10-12 10:50:45','',220,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1604',0,'revision','',0),(1605,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:45','2026-10-12 10:50:45','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Ask Lin Wei-Chen what Orbit Assembly does and the answer takes about eleven seconds. Ask what it decided not to do and the conversation runs for an hour. That ratio is, in Lin Wei-Chen’s view, the most important thing about the business.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Orbit Assembly operates in industrial robotics from Taipei with a product line roughly half the size of its nearest competitor’s. It reached that position by cutting, not by focusing from the start — three product lines were retired in a single year, along with the teams that had built them.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>Focus is not something you have. It is something you keep paying for, every quarter, against very good arguments.</p><cite>Lin Wei-Chen, co-founder and chief executive, Orbit Assembly</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1333,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-058579f56b-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1333\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Reported over several weeks and multiple conversations. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chennai_train_station.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Arian Zwegers\nderivative work: MrPanyGoff · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The cost of cutting</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The retirements were not costless. Orbit Assembly lost a meaningful share of revenue, two senior engineers and a great deal of goodwill among customers who had bought on the promise of a broader platform. Lin Wei-Chen spent most of a year in conversations that consisted largely of apologising and offering migration support.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>What the company got in return was an engineering organisation that could ship a significant release without a six-week regression cycle, and a sales team that could describe the product without qualification. Attachment rates on the remaining lines roughly doubled within eighteen months, largely because customers finally understood what they were buying.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the numbers show</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The clearest evidence is in the support data. Ticket volume per customer fell by more than half, and the composition changed: fewer questions about what the product does, more about how to use it well. Net revenue retention, which had been flat for three years, moved decisively for the first time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Cutting product lines is a customer-communication problem before it is an engineering one.</li>\n<li>Attachment rate is a better read on clarity than any brand metric.</li>\n<li>A narrower product changes the composition of support tickets, not just the volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Lin Wei-Chen is now under pressure — from the board, the sales organisation and a handful of large customers — to broaden the line again. The response so far has been a firm no, delivered with the weariness of someone who has had the argument many times and expects to have it again next quarter.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Lin Wei-Chen on the decision that defined Orbit Assembly','One call, made early, that everything since has been downstream of.','inherit','closed','closed','','222-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:45','2026-10-12 10:50:45','',222,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1605',0,'revision','',0),(1606,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:51','2026-10-12 10:50:51','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Kestrel Defence Systems took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Viktor Marchenko describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Warsaw business had a real problem: in aerospace components, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Viktor Marchenko, founder and chief executive, Kestrel Defence Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1327,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-61ae1687ee-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1327\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Skyline_From_Vidyasagar_Seti_2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Kestrel Defence Systems was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Viktor Marchenko regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Executive visibility without the performance','Being useful in public, at a cadence you can actually sustain.','inherit','closed','closed','','294-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:51','2026-10-12 10:50:51','',294,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1606',0,'revision','',0),(1607,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:52','2026-10-12 10:50:52','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Meridian Loop took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Priya Raghunathan describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Singapore business had a real problem: in logistics software, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Priya Raghunathan, co-founder and chief executive, Meridian Loop</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1329,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8cf0d28ab3-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1329\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DLF_Epitome_Tower,_DLF_Phase_3,_Gurugram.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Sahil Dhiman · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Meridian Loop was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Priya Raghunathan regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Buying committees grew. Meridian Loop rebuilt marketing around that.','Eleven people in the decision, and only one of them ever fills in a form.','inherit','closed','closed','','298-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:52','2026-10-12 10:50:52','',298,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1607',0,'revision','',0),(1608,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:52','2026-10-12 10:50:52','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Vesper Systems took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Tomas Novak describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Prague business had a real problem: in building automation, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Tomas Novak, chief operating officer, Vesper Systems</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1317,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-6fb7e0d1cc-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1317\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connaught_Place_sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Ville Miettinen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Vesper Systems was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Tomas Novak regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Search changed. Vesper Systems’s acquisition model had to change with it.','A rebuild of the demand engine, done while the numbers were still working.','inherit','closed','closed','','274-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:52','2026-10-12 10:50:52','',274,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1608',0,'revision','',0),(1609,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:52','2026-10-12 10:50:52','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Gregor Lindqvist publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Baltic Yield’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Malmö company sells into agricultural technology, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Gregor Lindqvist, chief executive, Baltic Yield</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1319,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-2a05fc14a4-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1319\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(37550).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Baltic Yield stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Gregor Lindqvist is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for agricultural technology businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Gregor Lindqvist on measuring marketing you cannot attribute','Giving up on perfect attribution turned out to improve the decisions.','inherit','closed','closed','','278-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:52','2026-10-12 10:50:52','',278,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1609',0,'revision','',0),(1610,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:53','2026-10-12 10:50:53','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meera Krishnan publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Aster Fieldworks’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Chennai company sells into field service software, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Meera Krishnan, founder, Aster Fieldworks</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1324,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-ae876be62e-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1324\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemon_Tree_hotel_in_HITEC_city.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">iMahesh · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Aster Fieldworks stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Meera Krishnan is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for field service software businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Meera Krishnan: “We stopped describing what we do.”','Describing who it is for turned out to be far more useful.','inherit','closed','closed','','288-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:53','2026-10-12 10:50:53','',288,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1610',0,'revision','',0),(1611,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:53','2026-10-12 10:50:53','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Ribera Foods could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Diego Salcedo has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Bogotá company operates in food distribution, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Diego Salcedo, co-founder and chief executive, Ribera Foods</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1323,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-687e93b3a9-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1323\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(98608).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Ribera Foods rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Diego Salcedo is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in food distribution are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','How Ribera Foods built a brand in an unbranded category','When competitors sound identical, being legible is the entire advantage.','inherit','closed','closed','','286-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:53','2026-10-12 10:50:53','',286,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1611',0,'revision','',0),(1612,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:53','2026-10-12 10:50:53','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Northgate Analytics took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Ethan Brightwell describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Manchester business had a real problem: in risk analytics, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Ethan Brightwell, co-founder, Northgate Analytics</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1332,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-dfb8103782-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1332\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ripon_Building_panorama.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">This Image was created by User:PlaneMad.\nIf you are using the image under the cr · CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Northgate Analytics was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Ethan Brightwell regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','ABM works. The version most companies run does not.','Account-based marketing without account-based selling is just expensive advertising.','inherit','closed','closed','','304-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:53','2026-10-12 10:50:53','',304,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1612',0,'revision','',0),(1613,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fatima Al-Rashid publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Nawa Health’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Dubai company sells into digital health, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Fatima Al-Rashid, co-founder, Nawa Health</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1326,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-7d34418678-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1326\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolkata_Central_business_district%27s_Skyline.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Innocentbunny · CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Nawa Health stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Fatima Al-Rashid is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for digital health businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Fatima Al-Rashid publishes twice a month. It generates half the pipeline.','A small, consistent output with an unusually high conversion rate.','inherit','closed','closed','','292-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','',292,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1613',0,'revision','',0),(1614,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebrand at Atelier Nove took eleven months, cost less than most companies spend on a single quarter of paid media, and involved almost no design work until month eight. Chiara Bellandi describes it, accurately, as a positioning project with a logo attached to the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Milan business had a real problem: in design manufacturing, its competitors described themselves in near-identical language, and buyers could not distinguish between them without a demo. That is an expensive problem, and it is a strategy problem rather than a visual one.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>We stopped trying to describe what we do. We started describing exactly who it is for, and the rest of it wrote itself.</p><cite>Chiara Bellandi, co-founder, Atelier Nove</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1320,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-5fed4cecd7-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1320\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Attention is the scarce resource; credibility is the durable one. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(47183).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Positioning before identity</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The work began with customer interviews — not the satisfaction kind, but structured conversations about what the alternative had been and why it was rejected. The pattern that emerged was narrower than anyone expected: Atelier Nove was consistently chosen by one specific type of buyer, for one specific reason, and almost never for the reasons in its own marketing.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Everything downstream followed from that. The website was rewritten around the buyer rather than the product. Pricing was restructured. Two features that had dominated the roadmap were deprioritised, because no customer had ever mentioned them as a reason to buy.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Most rebrands fail because they solve an identity problem the company does not have.</li>\n<li>Win-loss interviews are the cheapest positioning research available and the least used.</li>\n<li>If the roadmap does not change, the positioning work has not been done.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The logo, when it eventually arrived, took three weeks. Chiara Bellandi regards it as the least consequential part of the project and is mildly irritated that it is the part people ask about.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Content still works — just not the way it did','Volume stopped paying. Specificity started.','inherit','closed','closed','','280-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','',280,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1614',0,'revision','',0),(1615,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Trellis Property could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Oliver Ashworth has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Sydney company operates in commercial real estate, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Oliver Ashworth, chief executive, Trellis Property</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1325,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-60852483f6-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1325\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Positioning work rarely looks like marketing while it is happening. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(96390).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>What the reporting missed</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Volume held. Cost per acquisition rose modestly. What changed underneath was intent: a growing share of the demand was arriving earlier in the buying process, converting at a lower rate, and consuming considerably more sales attention per closed deal. Aggregate metrics showed a healthy channel; cohort analysis showed something else entirely.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The rebuild has been unglamorous. Trellis Property rebalanced towards channels that are harder to measure and easier to defend — a research programme, a small events series, and a deliberate investment in the kind of demand that never touches a form. Attribution got worse. The business got better.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>Aggregate channel metrics reliably hide compositional change in demand.</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis of pipeline quality surfaces problems two quarters before the totals do.</li>\n<li>Channels that resist attribution are not the same as channels that do not work.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Oliver Ashworth is honest that the new mix is harder to defend in a board meeting and harder to optimise. It is also, on the evidence of the last three quarters, producing better customers. That trade is one an increasing number of marketing leaders in commercial real estate are being asked to make.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','The founder profile that opens doors — and the one that closes them','Visibility is not the goal. Credibility with a specific audience is.','inherit','closed','closed','','290-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','',290,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1615',0,'revision','',0),(1616,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Samuel Adeyemi publishes about twice a month. There is no content calendar, no agency, and no attempt at volume. That output currently sources a substantial share of Longbridge Learning’s qualified pipeline, which is a fact worth examining carefully.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Nairobi company sells into workforce training, a market with perhaps a few thousand relevant buyers globally. In a market that size, reach is almost irrelevant and credibility with a specific audience is close to everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>I am not trying to be read widely. I am trying to be read by about four hundred people, correctly.</p><cite>Samuel Adeyemi, founder, Longbridge Learning</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1321,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-44848c3097-1200x675.jpg\" alt=\"Indian city skyline and business district\" class=\"wp-image-1321\" /><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Channels change faster than the buyers using them. <span class=\"cbw-credit-inline\">Photo: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitec_City,_Hyderabad_(80844).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">Syced · CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>Why narrow works</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The pieces are specific to the point of being unreadable for a general audience: operating detail, real numbers where they can be shared, and a willingness to describe things that did not work. That specificity is the mechanism. It is also why the output cannot be delegated, and why the cadence has to be modest.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The commercial effect is indirect and slow. Buyers arrive already convinced, having read three or four pieces over several months, and the sales conversation starts at a different point. Attribution captures almost none of this, which is why Longbridge Learning stopped trying to measure it that way and started asking buyers directly.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul>\n<li>In small markets, credibility with a few hundred people outperforms reach entirely.</li>\n<li>Specificity is what makes founder-authored content work, and it is what makes it undelegatable.</li>\n<li>Asking buyers how they arrived is more accurate than any attribution model at this scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Samuel Adeyemi is clear that this does not generalise. A company selling to a large, fragmented market would be badly served by twice-monthly essays. But for workforce training businesses with a countable set of buyers, the case for depth over volume is much stronger than the prevailing advice suggests.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n','Rebrands fail for boring reasons. Longbridge Learning avoided them.','Not a logo project: a positioning decision with an identity attached.','inherit','closed','closed','','282-revision-v1','','','2026-10-12 16:20:54','2026-10-12 10:50:54','',282,'http://localhost:8080/?p=1616',0,'revision','',0),(1617,0,'2026-10-12 16:20:55','2026-10-12 10:50:55','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>For six years, Halden Grid could describe its customer acquisition in one sentence. That sentence stopped being true about eighteen months ago, and Daniel Okonkwo has spent the time since rebuilding a machine that was, by most measures, still working.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The Lagos company operates in energy infrastructure, where a single dominant channel had reliably produced qualified demand at a stable cost. The cost stopped being stable. More importantly, the composition of what arrived changed in ways the reporting did not surface for two quarters.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><p>The dashboard was green the entire time the business was getting worse. That is the part I think about.</p><cite>Daniel Okonkwo, founder, Halden Grid</cite></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":1330,\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"http://localhost:8080/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/gms-in-city-8ce8266747-1200x67